// Medieval India c. 650–1760 (group 'his-med') — early medieval kingdoms, sultanates, Vijayanagara, Mughals,
// Marathas/Sikhs/regional states, Bhakti–Sufi centres, medieval battles. Coordinates checked against Wikipedia/Wikidata.

GEO.add({
 id:'his-earlymed', group:'his-med', title:'Early Medieval Kingdoms (c. 650–1200)', bn:'আদি-মধ্যযুগের রাজ্য', icon:'👑', world:false,
 intro:'ত্রিপাক্ষিক সংগ্রাম (পাল–প্রতিহার–রাষ্ট্রকূট) কনৌজ ঘিরে; দক্ষিণে চোল-চালুক্য-হোয়সল, উত্তরে রাজপুত — রাজবংশ ↔ রাজধানী ↔ মন্দির জোড়া মনে রাখো।',
 types:{tri:['Tripartite powers (Pala, Pratihara, Rashtrakuta)','#c62828'], south:['Southern dynasties (Chola, Chalukya, Pallava, Pandya, Hoysala, Kakatiya, Yadava, Ganga, Chera)','#1565c0'], raj:['Rajput kingdoms (Chauhan, Paramara, Chandela, Solanki, Kalachuri, Gahadavala)','#ef6c00'], east:['East, North-East & Kashmir','#2e7d32']},
 pts:[
  ['Kannauj — Pratihara & Gahadavala capital',27.031,79.922,'Uttar Pradesh','Prize of the Tripartite Struggle (8th–10th c.); Pratihara capital from Nagabhata II; later Gahadavalas (Govindachandra, Jaichand).','tri',
    { old:'Kanyakubja (ancient); Mahodaya / Kusumapura (epithets in inscriptions)',
      more:'Harsha (606–647) made Kanyakubja his capital; Xuanzang attended his assembly here (643). From the 8th c. the Palas, Gurjara-Pratiharas and Rashtrakutas fought the Tripartite Struggle for it; Nagabhata II made it the Pratihara capital. Under the Gahadavalas (11th–12th c.) it stayed a major city until Jaichand fell to Muhammad Ghori at Chandawar (1194). Today famous for attar (perfume).' }],
  ['Monghyr (Munger) — Pala camp-capital',25.381,86.465,'Bihar','Devapala\'s Monghyr copper plate issued from Mudgagiri camp; Palas also ruled from Pataliputra (Khalimpur plate of Dharmapala).','tri',
    { old:'Mudgagiri / Modagiri (Pala inscriptions); Mungir',
      more:'Devapala\'s Monghyr copper plate (9th c.) was issued from the victory camp at Mudgagiri. The Palas (Gopala c. 750, chosen by the nobles per the Khalimpur plate; Dharmapala; Devapala) were great patrons of Mahayana Buddhism. Munger fort later became Mir Qasim\'s capital (1762), where he set up a gun factory.' }],
  ['Manyakheta (Malkhed) — Rashtrakuta capital',17.195,77.161,'Karnataka (Kalaburagi)','Rashtrakuta capital from Amoghavarsha I (9th c.), author of Kavirajamarga; sacked by Paramara Siyaka c. 972.','tri',
    { old:'Manyakheta (Rashtrakuta) → Malkhed',
      more:'Amoghavarsha I (814–878) moved the Rashtrakuta capital here; he is credited with the Kannada poetics text Kavirajamarga and patronised Jain scholars like Jinasena. The Arab traveller Sulaiman counted the Rashtrakuta king among the four great kings of the world. The Paramara Siyaka sacked the city (c. 972); the Western Chalukyas then rose under Tailapa II.' }],
  ['Ellora — Kailasa temple (Cave 16)',20.024,75.179,'Maharashtra (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar)','Monolithic Kailasa temple cut under Rashtrakuta Krishna I (8th c.); Dantidurga\'s Dashavatara cave; UNESCO.','tri',
    { old:'Elapura (Rashtrakuta inscriptions); Verul (Marathi)',
      more:'34 rock-cut caves (c. 600–1000 CE): Buddhist (1–12), Hindu (13–29) and Jain (30–34) side by side — a symbol of religious tolerance. Cave 16, the Kailasa temple, was carved top-down from a single rock under Rashtrakuta Krishna I (c. 760). Dantidurga\'s inscription is in the Dashavatara cave (Cave 15). UNESCO World Heritage Site 1983.' }],
  ['Vikramashila Mahavihara',25.324,87.287,'Bihar (Antichak, Bhagalpur)','Founded by Pala king Dharmapala (c. 800); Vajrayana centre; Atisha Dipankara went to Tibet; destroyed c. 1200.','tri',
    { old:'Vikramashila (Pala); site at Antichak village',
      more:'Founded by Pala king Dharmapala (c. 800) as a rival to Nalanda; it specialised in Vajrayana (Tantric) Buddhism. Its famous teacher Atisha Dipankara Srijnana went to Tibet (1042) and reformed Tibetan Buddhism. Destroyed by Bakhtiyar Khalji\'s forces c. 1200. ASI excavations revealed a large cruciform stupa; a new central university is planned here.' }],
  ['Odantapuri (Bihar Sharif)',25.197,85.518,'Bihar (Nalanda)','Pala mahavihara attributed to Gopala/Dharmapala; model for Samye monastery in Tibet; sacked by Bakhtiyar Khalji.','tri',
    { old:'Odantapuri / Uddandapura (Pala); Bihar (from vihara) → Bihar Sharif',
      more:'Mahavihara attributed to Gopala or Dharmapala (8th c.), second in age after Nalanda. Tibetan tradition says the first Tibetan monastery, Samye (c. 775), was modelled on it. Bakhtiyar Khalji sacked it (c. 1193–1200), reportedly mistaking it for a fort; the town later took the name "Bihar" (from vihara), which gave the province its name.' }],
  ['Somapura Mahavihara (Paharpur)',25.031,88.977,'Bangladesh','Built by Pala king Dharmapala (late 8th c.); largest Buddhist vihara south of the Himalaya; UNESCO.','tri',
    { old:'Somapura Mahavihara (Pala); Paharpur ("hill-town")',
      more:'Built by Pala king Dharmapala (late 8th c.), it was a leading centre of Buddhist learning for about 300 years. Its cruciform terraced central shrine influenced temple plans in Myanmar and Java. Terracotta plaques line the walls. Located in Naogaon district, Bangladesh; UNESCO World Heritage Site 1985.' }],
  ['Thanjavur — Brihadeeswarar (Rajarajeswaram)',10.783,79.132,'Tamil Nadu','Imperial Chola capital (Vijayalaya c. 850); Rajaraja I completed Brihadeeswarar 1010; Great Living Chola Temples (UNESCO).','south',
    { old:'Tanjore (British); temple: Rajarajeswaram / Peruvudaiyar Kovil',
      more:'Vijayalaya Chola took Thanjavur from the Muttaraiyars c. 850 and made it the Chola capital. Rajaraja I completed the Brihadeeswarar (Rajarajeswaram) temple in 1010: a c. 66 m vimana, Chola frescoes and karana dance sculptures. It is one of the Great Living Chola Temples (UNESCO 1987, extended 2004). Later a Nayak and then Maratha capital.' }],
  ['Gangaikonda Cholapuram',11.205,79.453,'Tamil Nadu (Ariyalur)','Capital built by Rajendra I after his Ganga expedition (c. 1025); Chola-gangam tank; Great Living Chola Temples.','south',
    { old:'Gangaikonda Cholapuram ("city of the Chola who took the Ganga")',
      more:'Rajendra I (1014–1044) built this new capital after his northern expedition to the Ganga (c. 1019–25), taking the title Gangaikonda Chola. Its Brihadisvara temple (c. 1035) has a gracefully curved vimana; the huge Chola-gangam (Ponneri) tank was called a "liquid pillar of victory". Chola capital for about 250 years; part of the Great Living Chola Temples (UNESCO).' }],
  ['Darasuram — Airavatesvara temple',10.948,79.357,'Tamil Nadu (Thanjavur)','Built by Rajaraja II (12th c.); chariot-shaped mandapa; third of the Great Living Chola Temples (UNESCO).','south',
    { old:'Rajarajapuram (Chola-era name)',
      more:'The Airavatesvara temple was built by Rajaraja II (c. 1146–1173); its front mandapa is shaped as a chariot drawn by horses, and it has "musical" steps and fine miniature friezes. With Thanjavur and Gangaikonda Cholapuram it forms the Great Living Chola Temples (UNESCO). Darasuram lies near Kumbakonam.' }],
  ['Uttaramerur — Chola sabha inscriptions',12.617,79.753,'Tamil Nadu (Kanchipuram)','Parantaka I inscriptions (c. 919–921) on Vaikuntha Perumal temple: election of village sabha by kudavolai (pot-ticket).','south',
    { old:'Uttaramerur Chaturvedimangalam (brahmadeya village)',
      more:'Inscriptions of Parantaka I (c. 919 and 921) on the Vaikuntha Perumal temple wall lay down how the village sabha was elected by kudavolai (palm-leaf tickets drawn from a pot). They give qualifications (property, age 35–70, Vedic learning) and disqualifications for members of committees (variyams). Often cited as early evidence of local self-government.' }],
  ['Nagapattinam — Chola port & Chudamani Vihara',10.767,79.833,'Tamil Nadu','Chola port; Chudamani Vihara built by Srivijaya king Maravijayottungavarman under Rajaraja I\'s grant (Larger Leiden plates).','south',
    { old:'Negapatam (Dutch/British); Cholakula-vallipattinam (Chola)',
      more:'Chief Chola port for Southeast Asia. The Srivijaya (Sailendra) king Maravijayottungavarman built the Chudamani Vihara here with a village grant from Rajaraja I, recorded in the Larger Leiden copper plates. Rajendra I\'s naval expedition against Srivijaya (c. 1025) sailed from this coast. Later a Portuguese and then Dutch settlement (1658).' }],
  ['Kanchipuram — Pallava capital',12.842,79.690,'Tamil Nadu','Pallava capital; Kailasanatha temple of Rajasimha (c. 700); Vaikuntha Perumal (Nandivarman II); ghatika; later Chola city.','south',
    { old:'Kanchi / Kachchi (Sangam); Conjeeveram (British)',
      more:'Pallava capital (c. 4th–9th c.), "city of a thousand temples" and a seat of learning (ghatika). Rajasimha (Narasimhavarman II) built the Kailasanatha temple (c. 700); Nandivarman II built Vaikuntha Perumal. Xuanzang visited c. 640. Mahendravarman I wrote the play Mattavilasa Prahasana. Later Chola and Vijayanagara city; famous for silk saris (GI).' }],
  ['Madurai — Pandya capital',9.920,78.119,'Tamil Nadu','Capital of First & Later Pandya empires (Jatavarman Sundara Pandya, 13th c.); Marco Polo visited Pandya coast.','south',
    { old:'Madurai / Koodal (Sangam); Madura (British)',
      more:'Capital of the Pandyas, linked with the Sangam assemblies. The Later Pandyas peaked under Jatavarman Sundara Pandya (1251–68); Marco Polo visited the Pandya coast (c. 1292). Malik Kafur raided it (1311) and the Madurai Sultanate (1335–78) followed until Kumara Kampana of Vijayanagara ended it. The Madurai Nayaks built most of the present Meenakshi temple.' }],
  ['Vengi (Pedavegi) — Eastern Chalukyas',16.7708,81.1039,'Andhra Pradesh (Eluru)','Capital of Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi founded by Kubja Vishnuvardhana (624), brother of Pulakeshin II.','south',
    { old:'Vengi / Vengipura (capital of Vengi-desha)',
      more:'Ancient capital of the Salankayanas and then the Eastern Chalukyas, founded by Kubja Vishnuvardhana (624), brother of Pulakeshin II. Vengi, between the Krishna and Godavari, was fought over by Rashtrakutas, Western Chalukyas and Cholas; Chola marriage alliances led to Kulottunga I uniting both houses (1070). Mounds near Eluru.' }],
  ['Rajahmundry (Rajamahendravaram)',17.000,81.804,'Andhra Pradesh (East Godavari)','Later Eastern Chalukya capital of Rajaraja Narendra (11th c.); Nannaya began Telugu Mahabharata here.','south',
    { old:'Rajamahendrapuram (Eastern Chalukya) → Rajahmundry → Rajamahendravaram (2015)',
      more:'Named after Eastern Chalukya Rajaraja Narendra (1019–61), who ruled from here. His court poet Nannaya began the Telugu Mahabharata — the start of Telugu literature (Adikavi). Later held by Kakatiyas, Reddis, Gajapatis, Vijayanagara and Golconda. Officially renamed Rajamahendravaram in 2015; a Godavari Pushkaram site.' }],
  ['Kalyani (Basavakalyan) — Western Chalukyas',17.870,76.950,'Karnataka (Bidar)','Capital of Chalukyas of Kalyani (Someshvara I); Vikramaditya VI — Bilhana\'s Vikramankadevacharita, Vijnaneshwara\'s Mitakshara.','south',
    { old:'Kalyana / Kalyani (Chalukya) → Basavakalyan (renamed 1956 after Basava)',
      more:'Someshvara I (1042–68) made Kalyani the capital of the Western (Later) Chalukyas. Under Vikramaditya VI (1076–1126), Bilhana wrote Vikramankadevacharita and Vijnaneshwara wrote the Mitakshara, the basis of Hindu inheritance law. Kalachuri Bijjala later seized the city; his minister Basava led the Lingayat movement here.' }],
  ['Talakad — Western Ganga capital',12.220,77.030,'Karnataka (Mysuru)','Western Ganga capital on Kaveri; taken by Cholas (1004) and by Hoysala Vishnuvardhana (1116); now sand-buried temples.','south',
    { old:'Talavanapura (Ganga); Rajarajapura (Chola name after 1004)',
      more:'Capital of the Western Gangas on the Kaveri. Rajaraja Chola took it (1004) and renamed it Rajarajapura. Hoysala Vishnuvardhana recaptured it (1116), took the title Talakadugonda and built Belur\'s Chennakeshava temple to celebrate. Shifting sand dunes later buried many temples; the Panchalinga Darshana festival is held here.' }],
  ['Shravanabelagola — Gommateshwara',12.854,76.485,'Karnataka (Hassan)','17.8 m Bahubali monolith (c. 983) by Chavundaraya, minister of Ganga king Rachamalla IV; Mahamastakabhisheka.','south',
    { old:'Belgola / Shravana Belgola ("white pond of the shramanas")',
      more:'Jain tradition says Chandragupta Maurya came here with Bhadrabahu and died by sallekhana on Chandragiri hill. Chavundaraya, minister of Ganga king Rachamalla IV, set up the 17.8 m Gommateshwara (Bahubali) monolith on Vindhyagiri c. 983. The Mahamastakabhisheka is held about every 12 years. Its inscriptions are among the oldest in Kannada.' }],
  ['Dwarasamudra (Halebidu) — Hoysala capital',13.213,75.995,'Karnataka (Hassan)','Hoysala capital; Hoysaleswara temple (12th c.); sacked by Malik Kafur (1311); Sacred Ensembles of Hoysalas (UNESCO 2023).','south',
    { old:'Dorasamudra / Dwarasamudra (Hoysala) → Halebidu ("old capital")',
      more:'Hoysala capital from the 12th c.; the Hoysaleswara temple (c. 1121, under Vishnuvardhana) has dense soapstone friezes on a star-shaped plinth. Malik Kafur sacked it in 1311 and Tughlaq forces again in the 1320s–40s, after which it was abandoned — hence "Halebidu". Part of the Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas (UNESCO 2023).' }],
  ['Belur — Chennakeshava temple',13.163,75.861,'Karnataka (Hassan)','Early Hoysala capital; Chennakeshava built by Vishnuvardhana (1117) to mark victory over Cholas at Talakad; UNESCO 2023.','south',
    { old:'Velapura / Beluhur (Hoysala inscriptions)',
      more:'Early Hoysala capital on the Yagachi river. Vishnuvardhana built the Chennakeshava temple (1117), traditionally to mark his victory over the Cholas at Talakad. It is famous for madanika (bracket figure) sculptures and lathe-turned pillars. Part of the Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas (UNESCO 2023) with Halebidu and Somanathapura.' }],
  ['Warangal (Orugallu) — Kakatiya capital',17.957,79.614,'Telangana','Kakatiya capital (Ganapati, Rudramadevi, Prataparudra); Kakatiya Kala Thoranam; Marco Polo praised Rudramadevi.','south',
    { old:'Orugallu / Ekasilanagaram ("one-stone city"); Sultanpur (Tughlaq name, 1323)',
      more:'Kakatiya capital from Ganapati Deva (1199–1262), who moved it from Hanumakonda. Rani Rudramadevi (c. 1262–89) ruled as king; Marco Polo praised her. Prataparudra II submitted to Malik Kafur (1310), but Ulugh Khan annexed it in 1323 and renamed it Sultanpur. The Kakatiya Kala Thoranam gateway is in Telangana\'s state emblem.' }],
  ['Ramappa temple (Palampet)',18.259,79.942,'Telangana (Mulugu)','Kakatiya Rudreshwara temple built 1213 by general Recharla Rudra under Ganapati Deva; floating bricks; UNESCO 2021.','south',
    { old:'Rudreshwara temple (original name); Palampet village',
      more:'Built in 1213 by Kakatiya general Recharla Rudra under Ganapati Deva, and popularly named after its sculptor Ramappa — a rare temple named after an artisan. Its roof bricks are light enough to float; it stands on a sandbox foundation and has black-basalt bracket figures. Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2021.' }],
  ['Devagiri — Yadava capital',19.945,75.217,'Maharashtra (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar)','Seuna Yadava capital (Bhillama V, c. 1187; Ramachandra); Hemadri\'s Hemadpanti style; raided by Alauddin Khalji 1296.','south',
    { old:'Devagiri (Seuna Yadava) → Daulatabad (Muhammad bin Tughlaq, 1327)',
      more:'Bhillama V made Devagiri the Seuna Yadava capital c. 1187. Under Ramachandra, minister Hemadri wrote Chaturvarga Chintamani and is linked with the Hemadpanti temple style. Alauddin Khalji\'s raid (1296) was the first Turkish attack on the Deccan; Malik Kafur came again in 1307. The hill fort, with moat and dark spiral passage, later became Daulatabad.' }],
  ['Mahodayapuram (Kodungallur) — Chera Perumals',10.220,76.220,'Kerala (Thrissur)','Capital of Chera Perumals (Kulasekharas, 9th–12th c.); Kollam era began 825 CE; Cheraman Juma Masjid tradition.','south',
    { old:'Mahodayapuram / Makotai (Chera); Cranganore (European); Muziris port nearby',
      more:'Capital of the Chera Perumals of Kerala, c. 9th–12th c.; the Alvar saint Kulasekhara is traditionally counted a Chera king. Nearby Muziris was the Roman-era spice port. Tradition links the Cheraman Juma Masjid with the first mosque in India and St Thomas\'s arrival with this coast. The Kollam (Malayalam) era began in 825 CE. The 1341 flood silted the harbour.' }],
  ['Ajmer (Taragarh) — Chauhan capital',26.442,74.618,'Rajasthan','Ajayameru founded by Chauhan Ajayaraja II (early 12th c.); Vigraharaja IV\'s Sanskrit college is said to underlie Adhai Din ka Jhonpra.','raj',
    { old:'Ajayameru (Chauhan, 12th c.) → Ajmer',
      more:'Founded by Chauhan Ajayaraja II (early 12th c.); the Taragarh hill fort guarded it. Vigraharaja IV wrote the play Harakeli Nataka, inscribed on slabs from a Sanskrit college believed to underlie the Adhai Din ka Jhonpra mosque (Aibak, c. 1199). Prithviraj III ruled from here until Tarain (1192). Later famous for Moinuddin Chishti\'s dargah.' }],
  ['Shakambhari (Sambhar) — early Chauhan seat',26.910,75.186,'Rajasthan (Jaipur)','Early capital of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari (Sapadalaksha) before Ajmer; salt lake.','raj',
    { old:'Shakambhari (Chahamana capital); Sapadalaksha country → Sambhar',
      more:'Original seat of the Chahamanas (Chauhans) of Shakambhari, named after goddess Shakambhari, before Ajayaraja II founded Ajmer. The region was called Sapadalaksha ("1.25 lakh villages"). Sambhar Salt Lake, India\'s largest inland saline lake and a Ramsar site, has long been a major salt source.' }],
  ['Dhar — Paramara capital',22.599,75.303,'Madhya Pradesh','Paramara capital of Raja Bhoja (c. 1010–1055); Bhojshala Sanskrit school; Bhoja wrote Samarangana Sutradhara.','raj',
    { old:'Dhara / Dharanagari (Paramara)',
      more:'Bhoja (c. 1010–55) ruled the Paramara kingdom from Dhara. A polymath, he is credited with the Samarangana Sutradhara (architecture) and set up a Sanskrit school, the Bhojshala, with a Saraswati (Vagdevi) image now in the British Museum. The Bhojshala–Kamal Maula mosque complex remains a disputed site. Dhar later became a Malwa Sultanate and Maratha (Pawar) seat.' }],
  ['Bhojpur — Bhojeshwar temple',23.100,77.580,'Madhya Pradesh (Raisen)','Unfinished Shiva temple of Paramara Bhoja (11th c.) with one of the largest lingas; Bhoja\'s great dam/lake nearby.','raj',
    { old:'Bhojpur ("city of Bhoja")',
      more:'Unfinished Shiva temple of Paramara Bhoja (11th c.) with one of India\'s largest single-stone lingas. Plans engraved on nearby rocks and an earthen ramp reveal medieval building methods. Bhoja also built a great dam creating a huge lake south-east of Bhopal, later breached by Hoshang Shah of Malwa (traditional account).' }],
  ['Mount Abu — Dilwara temples',24.609,72.723,'Rajasthan (Sirohi)','Jain temples: Vimala Vasahi (1031, Vimala, minister of Solanki Bhima I) & Luna Vasahi (1230, Vastupala–Tejapala).','raj',
    { old:'Arbuda / Arbudachala (ancient name of Mount Abu)',
      more:'White-marble Jain temples: Vimala Vasahi (1031) built by Vimala Shah, minister of Solanki Bhima I, dedicated to Adinath; Luna Vasahi (1230) built by brothers Vastupala and Tejapala, ministers of the Vaghelas, dedicated to Neminath. Famous for intricate ceiling carving. Mount Abu, in the Aravallis, is Rajasthan\'s only hill station; Guru Shikhar is the Aravalli\'s highest peak.' }],
  ['Modhera — Sun temple',23.584,72.133,'Gujarat (Mehsana)','Surya temple built 1026–27 under Chaulukya (Solanki) Bhima I; stepped Surya Kund.','raj',
    { old:'Modhera (Modherak in Puranic tradition)',
      more:'Surya temple built 1026–27 under Chaulukya (Solanki) Bhima I, soon after Mahmud of Ghazni\'s Somnath raid. It has a garbhagriha, gudhamandapa, a sabhamandapa and the stepped tank Surya Kund with many small shrines, and is aligned so the equinox sunrise lights the shrine. Modhera was declared India\'s first solar-powered village (2022).' }],
  ['Anhilwara Patan — Solanki capital',23.859,72.102,'Gujarat (Patan)','Chaulukya (Solanki) capital; Rani ki Vav stepwell (Udayamati, 11th c.; UNESCO, on ₹100 note); sacked by Aibak 1197.','raj',
    { old:'Anahilapataka / Anahilwad (Chavda–Solanki); Nahrwala (Persian sources) → Patan',
      more:'Founded by Vanaraja Chavda (c. 746); capital of the Chaulukyas (Solankis) — Mularaja, Bhima I, Jayasimha Siddharaja and Kumarapala (patron of Hemachandra). Rani ki Vav stepwell was built by queen Udayamati in Bhima I\'s memory (UNESCO 2014; on the ₹100 note). Sacked by Aibak (1197) and Alauddin\'s army (1299). Famous for Patola silk.' }],
  ['Khajuraho — Chandela temples',24.850,79.933,'Madhya Pradesh (Chhatarpur)','Chandela temples c. 950–1050 (Lakshmana — Yashovarman; Kandariya Mahadeva — Vidyadhara); UNESCO 1986.','raj',
    { old:'Kharjuravahaka (inscriptions); Jejakabhukti (Chandela country)',
      more:'About 85 temples were built c. 950–1050, of which about 25 survive, in the Nagara style. The Lakshmana temple dates to Yashovarman (c. 954) and the Kandariya Mahadeva to Vidyadhara, who resisted Mahmud of Ghazni. Hindu and Jain temples stand together; erotic sculpture is only a small part. Ibn Battuta mentioned it. UNESCO World Heritage Site 1986.' }],
  ['Mahoba — Chandela capital',25.280,79.873,'Uttar Pradesh','Chandela capital; Alha–Udal ballads; Prithviraj Chauhan defeated Paramardi (1182); Madan Sagar lake.','raj',
    { old:'Mahotsavanagara (Chandela, traditional) → Mahoba',
      more:'Chandela capital, named traditionally after a great festival (mahotsava) of the dynasty\'s founder. Ballads of Alha and Udal, generals of Paramardi, recount the war with Prithviraj Chauhan, who defeated Paramardi in 1182. Madan Sagar and Kirat Sagar tanks were built by Chandela kings. Qutbuddin Aibak took the region in 1203.' }],
  ['Kalinjar Fort',25.000,80.485,'Uttar Pradesh (Banda)','Chandela stronghold besieged by Mahmud (1023); taken by Aibak 1203; Sher Shah killed by gunpowder blast here (1545).','raj',
    { old:'Kalanjara (Puranic & Chandela)',
      more:'Vindhyan hill fort held by the Chandelas, who bought off Mahmud of Ghazni (1023) with tribute. Aibak took it in 1203. Sher Shah Suri was mortally wounded here by an exploding gunpowder charge while besieging it (1545). Akbar later held it (traditionally as Birbal\'s jagir). The Nilkanth temple inside has rock-cut sculptures.' }],
  ['Tripuri (Tewar) — Kalachuri capital',23.143,79.847,'Madhya Pradesh (Jabalpur)','Capital of Kalachuris of Tripuri (Gangeyadeva, Lakshmi-Karna, 11th c.) near Jabalpur.','raj',
    { old:'Tripuri (Kalachuri-Chedi capital); Chedi / Dahala country',
      more:'Capital of the Kalachuris of Tripuri (Chedi/Dahala) near Jabalpur. Gangeyadeva (c. 1015–41) took the title Vikramaditya, and his son Lakshmi-Karna defeated the Paramaras, Chandelas and Palas. They used the Kalachuri-Chedi era (from 248 CE). Ruins and sculptures survive at Tewar village.' }],
  ['Gwalior Fort',26.230,78.169,'Madhya Pradesh','Mihira Bhoja\'s Gwalior prashasti (Pratihara); Teli ka Mandir; Kachchhapaghata Sas–Bahu temples; Man Singh Tomar palace.','raj',
    { old:'Gopachala / Gopadri (inscriptions); Gopagiri',
      more:'Hill fort held by the Pratiharas, Kachchhapaghatas and Tomaras. Mihira Bhoja\'s Gwalior prashasti records the Pratihara line, and the Chaturbhuj temple inscription (876) has one of the earliest written zeros. Teli ka Mandir, the Sas–Bahu temples (1093) and Raja Man Singh Tomar\'s Man Mandir palace (c. 1500) survive. Babur called it the pearl among the fortresses of Hind.' }],
  ['Kalinganagar (Mukhalingam) — Eastern Ganga',18.600,83.967,'Andhra Pradesh (Srikakulam)','Early capital of Eastern Gangas of Kalinga; Madhukeshvara temple; later capital moved to Cuttack.','east',
    { old:'Kalinganagara (Eastern Ganga capital) → Mukhalingam',
      more:'Early capital of the Eastern Gangas of Kalinga. The Madhukeshvara temple (c. 9th c.) is an early Kalinga-style temple with rich carving; Someshvara and Bhimeshvara temples are nearby. Anantavarman Chodaganga (1078–1147) later moved the capital to Cuttack and began the Jagannath temple at Puri.' }],
  ['Konark — Sun temple',19.887,86.095,'Odisha (Puri)','Chariot-shaped Surya temple of Eastern Ganga Narasimhadeva I (c. 1250); "Black Pagoda"; UNESCO; on ₹10 note.','east',
    { old:'Konaditya / Arka-kshetra; "Black Pagoda" (European sailors)',
      more:'Built by Eastern Ganga king Narasimhadeva I (c. 1250) as a colossal chariot of Surya with 24 carved wheels drawn by seven horses. The main tower collapsed; the jagamohana survives (filled with sand by the British in 1903). The Konark wheel appears on the ₹10 note; UNESCO 1984. The Konark Dance Festival is held each December.' }],
  ['Lakhnauti (Gaur) — Sena capital',24.869,88.131,'West Bengal (Malda)','Sena capital (Ballala & Lakshmana Sena); taken by Bakhtiyar Khalji after Nadia raid (1204) — first Turkish base in Bengal.','east',
    { old:'Gauda / Lakshmanavati (Sena) → Lakhnauti → Jannatabad (Humayun, 1538)',
      more:'Capital of Gauda (Bengal) under the Palas and Senas; Lakshmana Sena gave it the name Lakshmanavati. Bakhtiyar Khalji surprised Lakshmana Sena at Nadia (c. 1204) and made Lakhnauti the first Turkish capital in Bengal. Humayun renamed it Jannatabad (1538). Plague and a shift in the Ganga\'s course led to its abandonment in 1575.' }],
  ['Pragjyotishpura (Guwahati) — Kamarupa',26.181,91.750,'Assam (Kamrup Metro)','Capital of Kamarupa (Varman dynasty — Bhaskaravarman, Harsha\'s ally); Kamakhya; Mlechchha & Pala dynasties followed.','east',
    { old:'Pragjyotishpura (Epic / Kamarupa); Gauhati (British) → Guwahati',
      more:'Legendary capital of Narakasura and capital of the Kamarupa kingdom. The Varman king Bhaskaravarman (7th c.) allied with Harsha and hosted Xuanzang. Kamakhya temple on Nilachal hill is a major Shakti pitha. The Mlechchha and Pala dynasties of Kamarupa followed. The Ahoms defeated the Mughals near here at Saraighat (1671).' }],
  ['Haruppeshvara (Tezpur) — Kamarupa',26.620,92.790,'Assam (Sonitpur)','Haruppeshvara, capital of the Mlechchha (Salastambha) dynasty of Kamarupa; Gupta-style Da-Parbatia door frame nearby.','east',
    { old:'Haruppeshvara (Mlechchha capital); Sonitpur (Puranic legend)',
      more:'Capital of the Mlechchha (Salastambha) dynasty of Kamarupa (c. 7th–10th c.); legend links Tezpur (Sonitpur) with king Bana and the Usha–Aniruddha story at Agnigarh hill. The Da-Parbatia door frame (c. 5th–6th c.) nearby, with Ganga and Yamuna figures in Gupta style, is among the oldest sculpture in Assam.' }],
  ['Parihaspora — Lalitaditya\'s capital',34.133,74.633,'Jammu & Kashmir (Baramulla)','Built by Karkota king Lalitaditya Muktapida (8th c.); stupa & chaitya ruins; Kalhana\'s Rajatarangini source.','east',
    { old:'Parihasapura ("city of laughter", Karkota) → Paraspor',
      more:'Built by Karkota king Lalitaditya Muktapida (c. 724–760) as his capital, with a great stupa, chaitya and monastery raised by his minister Chankuna. Kalhana\'s Rajatarangini (1148) describes it. Lalitaditya\'s campaigns reached Kannauj, where he defeated Yashovarman. The ruins near Baramulla were later quarried for stone.' }],
  ['Martand Sun temple',33.745,75.220,'Jammu & Kashmir (Anantnag)','Built by Karkota Lalitaditya Muktapida (8th c.); Kashmiri style with Gandharan/Gupta elements; destroyed by Sikandar.','east',
    { old:'Martanda (Sun temple); Mattan village nearby',
      more:'Built by Karkota king Lalitaditya Muktapida (8th c.) on a plateau near Anantnag, dedicated to Surya (Martanda). It shows the Kashmiri style — trefoil arches, fluted columns and a colonnaded courtyard — blending Gandharan, Gupta and Greek elements. It was destroyed on the orders of Sultan Sikandar Butshikan (early 15th c.).' }],
  ['Avantipora — Utpala capital',33.923,75.013,'Jammu & Kashmir (Pulwama)','Founded by Utpala king Avantivarman (855–883); Avantiswami (Vishnu) temple; engineer Suyya\'s flood works.','east',
    { old:'Avantipura (founded by Avantivarman)',
      more:'Founded by Avantivarman (855–883), first king of the Utpala dynasty of Kashmir, who built the Avantiswami (Vishnu) and Avantishvara (Shiva) temples here. His engineer Suyya drained marshes and regulated the Jhelum, expanding farmland; Kalhana says grain prices fell sharply. The ruins lie on the Srinagar–Jammu highway.' }]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-sultanate', group:'his-med', title:'Delhi Sultanate & Regional Sultanates', bn:'দিল্লি সুলতানি ও আঞ্চলিক সুলতানি', icon:'🕌', world:false,
 intro:'দিল্লির শহরগুলোর ক্রম (কিলা রাই পিথোরা → সিরি → তুঘলকাবাদ → জাহানপনাহ → ফিরোজাবাদ), প্রাদেশিক সুলতানি আর বাহমনি ভেঙে পাঁচ দাক্ষিণাত্য সুলতানি — প্রিলিমসের প্রিয় প্রশ্ন।',
 types:{delhi:['Delhi Sultanate & Sur sites','#6a1b9a'], prov:['Provincial sultanates','#00838f'], deccan:['Bahmani & Deccan sultanates','#2e7d32'], battle:['Battle','#c62828']},
 pts:[
  ['Qila Rai Pithora & Qutb complex (Mehrauli)',28.524,77.185,'Delhi','Tomara Lal Kot / Chauhan Qila Rai Pithora; Aibak\'s Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque; Qutb Minar begun by Aibak, done by Iltutmish.','delhi',
    { old:'Lal Kot (Tomara, c. 1060) → Qila Rai Pithora (Chauhan); Mihirawali → Mehrauli',
      more:'Tomara Anangpal built Lal Kot; Prithviraj Chauhan expanded it as Qila Rai Pithora — the "first city of Delhi". Aibak built the Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque (1193) from temple spolia, around the 4th-c. Gupta iron pillar (Chandra inscription). Qutb Minar was begun by Aibak, finished by Iltutmish and repaired by Firuz Tughlaq; Alai Darwaza (1311) has the first true arches. UNESCO 1993.' }],
  ['Siri & Jahanpanah',28.552,77.224,'Delhi','Siri — 2nd city, Alauddin Khalji (1303) vs Mongols; Jahanpanah walls (Muhammad bin Tughlaq) linked Siri to Qila Rai Pithora.','delhi',
    { old:'Siri / Dar-ul-Khilafat (Alauddin, 1303); Jahanpanah ("refuge of the world", Muhammad bin Tughlaq)',
      more:'Alauddin Khalji built Siri (c. 1303), the second city of Delhi, as a camp-fortress against Mongol invasions; legend says Mongol heads were buried in its walls. He also built the Hauz-i-Alai (Hauz Khas) tank. Muhammad bin Tughlaq\'s Jahanpanah (c. 1326–27) walled in the area between Siri and Qila Rai Pithora; Bijay Mandal and Begumpur mosque survive.' }],
  ['Tughlaqabad',28.515,77.260,'Delhi','3rd city, Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (1321); his tomb opposite; Nizamuddin\'s legendary curse; abandoned soon after.','delhi',
    { old:'Tughlaqabad (Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq, 1321)',
      more:'Third city of Delhi, a massive rubble-stone fort built quickly by Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq (1320–25). His tomb, with sloping walls and a white-marble dome, stands on an artificial island linked by a causeway. Legend says Nizamuddin Auliya cursed it: "Ya rahe ujar, ya base Gujar". Ghiyasuddin died when a pavilion collapsed at Afghanpur (1325), and the fort was soon deserted.' }],
  ['Firozabad (Feroz Shah Kotla)',28.635,77.243,'Delhi','5th city of Firuz Shah Tughlaq (1354) on Yamuna; Topra Ashokan pillar mounted on pyramid.','delhi',
    { old:'Firozabad (5th city of Delhi, 1354); Kotla Firoz Shah',
      more:'Firuz Shah Tughlaq (1351–88) built Firozabad on the Yamuna as the fifth city of Delhi. He moved an Ashokan pillar from Topra (Ambala) and set it on a three-storey pyramidal structure; a second came from Meerut to the Ridge. The pillar\'s Brahmi edicts were deciphered by James Prinsep (1837). Firuz also built canals, sarais and the Hauz Khas madrasa.' }],
  ['Lodi Garden — Sayyid & Lodi tombs',28.593,77.220,'Delhi','Tombs of Muhammad Shah Sayyid & Sikandar Lodi (early double dome, garden tomb); Bara Gumbad (1490).','delhi',
    { old:'Khairpur village (old name); Lady Willingdon Park (1936) → Lodi Gardens',
      more:'Holds the octagonal tomb of Muhammad Shah Sayyid (1444), the tomb of Sikandar Lodi (1517) — an early example of a double dome and a walled garden tomb — and the Bara Gumbad (1490) with its mosque and Sheesh Gumbad. These Sayyid–Lodi octagonal tombs prefigured Humayun\'s tomb. Laid out as a park by the British in 1936.' }],
  ['Hisar — Hisar-e-Firoza',29.160,75.727,'Haryana','Founded by Firuz Shah Tughlaq (1354) with canals from Yamuna; Lat ki Masjid with Ashokan-era pillar.','delhi',
    { old:'Hisar-e-Firoza ("fort of Firuz", 1354) → Hisar',
      more:'Founded by Firuz Shah Tughlaq in 1354 with a fort and canals (Western Yamuna canal system) bringing water to this dry tract. The Lat ki Masjid in the Gujari Mahal complex holds an earlier pillar re-used by Firuz. Agroha, the ancestral seat of the Agarwals, is nearby. Hisar later gave its name to a Mughal sarkar.' }],
  ['Kara — Alauddin\'s coup',25.700,81.350,'Uttar Pradesh (Kaushambi)','Alauddin, governor of Kara, murdered his uncle Jalaluddin Khalji here (1296) after Devagiri raid.','delhi',
    { old:'Kara / Kara-Manikpur (iqta of the Sultanate)',
      more:'Kara on the Ganga was an important iqta. As its governor, Alauddin Khalji raided Devagiri (1296) and brought back huge treasure; he then invited his uncle Sultan Jalaluddin Khalji to Kara and had him murdered on the riverbank (July 1296), seizing the throne. Kara also has the dargah of Sufi saint Khwaja Karak.' }],
  ['Sasaram — Sher Shah\'s tomb',24.948,84.009,'Bihar (Rohtas)','Sur ruler Sher Shah (1540–45) — rupiya, Grand Trunk Road, sarais; tank-set octagonal tomb.','delhi',
    { old:'Sahasram (traditional)',
      more:'Sher Shah Suri (born Farid, c. 1486) held Sasaram as a jagir. As emperor (1540–45) he issued the silver rupiya, built the Grand Trunk Road (Sadak-e-Azam) with sarais, and reformed land revenue (patta, qabuliyat). His octagonal tomb (1545), built by architect Aliwal Khan, rises from an artificial lake — a high point of Indo-Afghan architecture.' }],
  ['Daulatabad — capital shift (1327)',19.945,75.217,'Maharashtra (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar)','Muhammad bin Tughlaq renamed Devagiri as Daulatabad, shifted capital 1327; Chand Minar (Bahmani, 1445).','delhi',
    { old:'Devagiri (Yadava) → Daulatabad ("city of fortune", 1327); Qubbat-ul-Islam',
      more:'Muhammad bin Tughlaq renamed Devagiri as Daulatabad and in 1327 ordered Delhi\'s elite to move here to control the Deccan; the plan failed and the court returned c. 1335. The Bahmani revolt (1347) began nearby. The Bahmanis built the Chand Minar (1445); later the Nizam Shahs and Mughals held it. Ibn Battuta described the move.' }],
  ['Badaun',28.040,79.120,'Uttar Pradesh','Iltutmish was governor here before becoming Sultan; last Sayyid Alam Shah retired to Badaun (1451); birthplace of Nizamuddin Auliya.','delhi',
    { old:'Budaun / Vodamayuta (inscriptions); Bedamuth (trad.)',
      more:'An important iqta under the early Sultans: Iltutmish governed it before becoming Sultan (1211), and his Shamsi Idgah and Jama Masjid survive. The last Sayyid Sultan Alam Shah retired here (1451), leaving Delhi to Bahlul Lodi. Birthplace of Nizamuddin Auliya (1238) and home of historian Abdul Qadir Badauni.' }],
  ['Tarain (1191 & 1192)',29.780,76.940,'Haryana (Karnal)','1191 Prithviraj Chauhan III defeated Muhammad Ghori; 1192 Ghori won — foundation of Turkish rule in north India.','battle',
    { old:'Tarain / Tarori → Taraori (Karnal district)',
      more:'In the First Battle of Tarain (1191) Prithviraj Chauhan III defeated Muhammad Ghori, who was wounded. In the Second Battle (1192) Ghori used mounted archers and feigned retreat to rout the Rajput army; Prithviraj was captured and killed. This laid the foundation of Turkish rule in North India, consolidated by Qutbuddin Aibak. Described in Prithviraj Raso and Tabaqat-i-Nasiri.' }],
  ['Chandawar (1194)',27.107,78.370,'Uttar Pradesh (Firozabad)','Muhammad Ghori (with Aibak) defeated Gahadavala Jaichand on the Yamuna; Kannauj–Varanasi opened to Turks.','battle',
    { old:'Chandwar / Chandawar (near Firozabad on the Yamuna)',
      more:'Muhammad Ghori, with Qutbuddin Aibak, defeated the Gahadavala king Jaichand (Jayachandra) of Kannauj in 1194; Jaichand was killed by an arrow. The victory opened the Gangetic plain — Kannauj, Varanasi and Asni — to Turkish control. Later legend made Jaichand a traitor, though this is not supported by contemporary sources.' }],
  ['Talikota / Rakkasagi–Tangadagi (1565)',16.473,76.312,'Karnataka (Vijayapura)','Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda & Bidar crushed Vijayanagara; Rama Raya killed; Hampi sacked.','battle',
    { old:'Battle of Rakkasa-Tangadi (Rakkasagi & Tangadagi villages near Talikota)',
      more:'On 23 January 1565 the allied Deccan Sultanates of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar crushed Vijayanagara under Aliya Rama Raya, who was captured and beheaded. The defection of Muslim commanders in Vijayanagara\'s army is cited as a cause. The capital Hampi was sacked for months; Tirumala moved the court to Penukonda. The battle was fought on the Krishna, about 25 km from Talikota.' }],
  ['Jaunpur — Sharqi Sultanate',25.753,82.690,'Uttar Pradesh','Founded by Firuz Tughlaq; Malik Sarwar (Malik-us-Sharq) set up Sharqi kingdom 1394; Atala mosque 1408; "Shiraz of India".','prov',
    { old:'Jaunpur (named by Firuz Tughlaq after Juna Khan / Muhammad bin Tughlaq); "Shiraz-i-Hind"',
      more:'Founded by Firuz Shah Tughlaq (c. 1359). Khwaja Jahan Malik Sarwar, titled Malik-us-Sharq, founded the Sharqi Sultanate (1394) after Timur\'s invasion weakened Delhi. Ibrahim Shah Sharqi built the Atala Masjid (1408), with a huge propylon screen; the Jama Masjid followed. A centre of Persian learning, it was annexed by Bahlul Lodi (1479).' }],
  ['Mandu — Malwa Sultanate',22.352,75.398,'Madhya Pradesh (Dhar)','Shadiabad of Malwa Sultans (Hoshang Shah, Ghiyas Shah); Jahaz Mahal, Hoshang\'s marble tomb; Baz Bahadur–Rupmati.','prov',
    { old:'Mandavagarh (Paramara); Shadiabad ("city of joy", Malwa Sultans)',
      more:'Dilawar Khan Ghuri founded the Malwa Sultanate (1401); his son Hoshang Shah moved the capital to Mandu. Hoshang Shah\'s tomb is India\'s first marble structure (said to have inspired the Taj). Ghiyas Shah built the Jahaz Mahal for his harem. Baz Bahadur and Rani Rupmati\'s romance ended with Akbar\'s conquest (1561). Rewa Kund and Hindola Mahal survive.' }],
  ['Ahmedabad — Gujarat Sultanate',23.024,72.581,'Gujarat','Founded 1411 by Ahmad Shah I on Sabarmati; Bhadra fort, Jama Masjid, Sidi Saiyyed jali; later Mughal Gujarat subah capital.','prov',
    { old:'Ashaval / Karnavati (pre-Sultanate) → Ahmedabad (1411); "Amdavad"',
      more:'Ahmad Shah I founded the city in 1411 on the Sabarmati near old Ashaval/Karnavati, with the Bhadra fort and Teen Darwaza. The Jama Masjid (1424) and the Sidi Saiyyed mosque\'s "tree of life" jali (1573) are famous. Akbar annexed Gujarat in 1572–73. The walled city became India\'s first UNESCO World Heritage City (2017).' }],
  ['Sarkhej Roza',22.992,72.505,'Gujarat (Ahmedabad)','Tomb of Sufi Shaikh Ahmad Khattu Ganj Bakhsh & Mahmud Begada; "Acropolis of Ahmedabad".','prov',
    { old:'Sarkhej (village near Ahmedabad)',
      more:'Complex around the tomb of Sufi saint Shaikh Ahmad Khattu Ganj Bakhsh (d. 1445), adviser to Ahmad Shah I. Mahmud Begada added palaces, a mosque and a large tank, and he and his queen Rajbai are buried here. It blends Islamic, Hindu and Jain styles; Le Corbusier reportedly called it the "Acropolis of Ahmedabad".' }],
  ['Champaner — Mahmud Begada',22.483,73.533,'Gujarat (Panchmahal)','Mahmud Begada took it 1484 & made it capital (Muhammadabad); Jami Masjid; Humayun took it 1535; UNESCO 2004.','prov',
    { old:'Champaner (Khichi Chauhan) → Muhammadabad (Mahmud Begada, 1484)',
      more:'Mahmud Begada took Champaner-Pavagadh from the Khichi Chauhans in 1484 after a 20-month siege, renamed it Muhammadabad and made it the Gujarat capital. Its Jami Masjid (1513) is a model of Indo-Islamic architecture. Humayun scaled the fort in 1535, after which it declined. Pavagadh hill has the Kalika Mata temple. UNESCO World Heritage Site 2004.' }],
  ['Khambhat (Cambay)',22.300,72.620,'Gujarat (Anand)','Chief port of Gujarat Sultanate; Malik Kafur bought here (1299); Jama Masjid 1325; silted gulf.','prov',
    { old:'Stambhatirtha (Sanskrit); Cambay (European)',
      more:'Chief port of medieval Gujarat for trade with the Persian Gulf and Red Sea; Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta described it. In 1299 Nusrat Khan bought the slave Malik Kafur here during Alauddin Khalji\'s Gujarat campaign. The Jama Masjid (1325) reuses temple parts. Siltation of the Gulf of Khambhat led to decline as Surat rose. Famous for agate beads.' }],
  ['Gaur — Bengal Sultanate',24.883,88.128,'West Bengal (Malda)','Capital of Bengal Sultans (Ilyas Shahi, Husain Shahi); Bara Sona Masjid (Nusrat Shah, 1526); Dakhil Darwaza.','prov',
    { old:'Gauda / Lakhnauti (early Sultanate) → Jannatabad (Humayun, 1538)',
      more:'Capital of the Bengal Sultanate for long periods (Ilyas Shahi, Husain Shahi). Alauddin Husain Shah (1494–1519) was a patron of Bengali literature. Monuments: Bara Sona Masjid (completed by Nusrat Shah, 1526), Dakhil Darwaza, Firoz Minar and Qadam Rasul. Humayun renamed it Jannatabad; plague (1575) led to its abandonment.' }],
  ['Pandua — Adina mosque',25.153,88.164,'West Bengal (Malda)','Ilyas Shahi capital; Adina mosque built 1373–75 by Sikandar Shah — largest mosque of the subcontinent then.','prov',
    { old:'Pandua / Firozabad (Ilyas Shahi capital)',
      more:'Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah (1342–58) united Bengal and made Pandua (called Firozabad) his capital; Firuz Tughlaq twice failed to subdue Bengal. His son Sikandar Shah built the Adina mosque (1373–75), then the largest mosque in the subcontinent, with a vast hypostyle prayer hall. Eklakhi mausoleum and the Qutb Shahi mosque survive.' }],
  ['Sonargaon',23.650,90.617,'Bangladesh','Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah\'s seat (1338); Ibn Battuta visited; Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah (corresponded with Hafiz) buried here.','prov',
    { old:'Suvarnagrama (ancient); Sonargaon ("golden village"); Panam Nagar (later quarter)',
      more:'Capital of eastern Bengal; Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah declared independence here (1338). Ibn Battuta visited in 1346. Sultan Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah, who corresponded with the poet Hafiz and sent embassies to Ming China, is buried here. Sonargaon was famous for fine muslin; the Sher Shah road ran from here to the Indus. Isa Khan of the Baro-Bhuiyans resisted Akbar from here.' }],
  ['Srinagar — Shah Mir Sultanate',34.098,74.809,'Jammu & Kashmir','Shah Mir dynasty from 1339; Sikandar Butshikan built Jamia Masjid (1402); Zain-ul-Abidin "Budshah"; Mughal Kashmir subah 1586.','prov',
    { old:'Pravarapura (founded by Pravarasena II, 6th c.); Srinagari',
      more:'Shah Mir founded the Kashmir Sultanate in 1339. Sikandar (Butshikan) built the Jamia Masjid (1402) and destroyed temples, while Zain-ul-Abidin (1420–70), called "Budshah", was tolerant, recalled Pandits, had the Mahabharata and Rajatarangini translated into Persian and promoted crafts. Akbar annexed Kashmir in 1586 and built the Hari Parbat wall.' }],
  ['Burhanpur — Faruqi Khandesh',21.300,76.227,'Madhya Pradesh','Faruqi capital of Khandesh on Tapti; Akbar annexed 1601 (Asirgarh); Mughal Deccan base; Mumtaz Mahal died here 1631.','prov',
    { old:'Burhanpur (founded c. 1399 by Nasir Khan Faruqi, named after Sufi Burhanuddin Gharib)',
      more:'Capital of the Faruqi kingdom of Khandesh on the Tapti; nearby Asirgarh was its fortress. Akbar annexed Khandesh in 1601. Burhanpur became the main Mughal base for Deccan campaigns; Mumtaz Mahal died here in childbirth (1631) and was first buried at Zainabad. Its Kundi Bhandara is a Mughal underground water system. Also a Dawoodi Bohra centre.' }],
  ['Gulbarga — first Bahmani capital',17.340,76.831,'Karnataka (Kalaburagi)','Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah founded Bahmani kingdom 1347; Jama Masjid in fort (1367) has no open courtyard.','deccan',
    { old:'Kalaburagi (Kannada) → Ahsanabad (Bahmani name) → Gulbarga; Kalaburagi (2014)',
      more:'Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah (Hasan Gangu) founded the Bahmani Sultanate in 1347 after revolting against Muhammad bin Tughlaq, and named Gulbarga Ahsanabad. The Jama Masjid in the fort (1367) is unusual in having its whole court covered by domes. Firuz Shah Bahmani brought the Sufi Gesu Daraz here. Capital moved to Bidar in 1429.' }],
  ['Bidar — Bahmani & Barid Shahi',17.922,77.524,'Karnataka','Ahmad Shah Wali shifted Bahmani capital here (1429); Mahmud Gawan madrasa (1472); later Barid Shahi capital; Bidriware.','deccan',
    { old:'Bidar → Muhammadabad (Bahmani name, 1429)',
      more:'Ahmad Shah I Wali moved the Bahmani capital here in 1429. Prime minister Mahmud Gawan built a three-storey madrasa (1472) in Persian style and reformed administration, but was executed in 1481 — the kingdom then broke up. Bidar became the Barid Shahi capital (1492–1619). Bidriware (silver inlay on zinc-copper alloy) has a GI tag. Russian traveller Afanasy Nikitin visited.' }],
  ['Bijapur — Adil Shahi',16.830,75.736,'Karnataka (Vijayapura)','Yusuf Adil Shah (1489); Ibrahim II\'s Kitab-i-Nauras; Gol Gumbaz of Muhammad Adil Shah — whispering gallery; fell 1686.','deccan',
    { old:'Vijayapura (Chalukya) → Bijapur → Vijayapura (2014)',
      more:'Yusuf Adil Shah founded the Adil Shahi dynasty in 1489. Ibrahim Adil Shah II (1580–1627), called "Jagadguru", wrote Kitab-i-Nauras in Dakhni. Gol Gumbaz (1656), tomb of Muhammad Adil Shah, has one of the world\'s largest domes and a whispering gallery. Ibrahim Rauza and the Malik-e-Maidan cannon survive. Aurangzeb annexed Bijapur in 1686.' }],
  ['Golconda — Qutb Shahi',17.383,78.404,'Telangana (Hyderabad)','Quli Qutb Shah (1518); diamond trade (Kollur); Muhammad Quli founded Hyderabad & Charminar 1591; fell to Aurangzeb 1687.','deccan',
    { old:'Golla Konda ("shepherd\'s hill", Kakatiya mud fort); Muhammadnagar',
      more:'Quli Qutb Shah declared independence from the Bahmanis in 1518. Golconda was the centre of the diamond trade (Kollur mines — Koh-i-Noor, Hope diamond tradition). Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah, a Dakhni poet, founded Hyderabad and built the Charminar (1591). Its acoustics system and Qutb Shahi tombs are famous. Aurangzeb took it in 1687 after an eight-month siege.' }],
  ['Ahmadnagar — Nizam Shahi',19.095,74.755,'Maharashtra (Ahilyanagar)','Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah founded city 1494; Chand Bibi defended against Mughals (1595); fell 1600; Malik Ambar later.','deccan',
    { old:'Ahmadnagar (founded 1494 by Ahmad Nizam Shah) → Ahilyanagar (2024)',
      more:'Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah founded the city in 1494 near Bhingar. Chand Bibi defended Ahmadnagar against Akbar\'s army (1595–96) but the fort fell in 1600. Malik Ambar then ruled a Nizam Shahi remnant from Khadki (Aurangabad), using guerrilla tactics and a revenue system later imitated by the Marathas. The fort later held Congress leaders, where Nehru wrote The Discovery of India (1942–45).' }],
  ['Ellichpur (Achalpur) — Imad Shahi Berar',21.257,77.509,'Maharashtra (Amravati)','Capital of Imad Shahi Berar (1490–1574), absorbed by Ahmadnagar; later Mughal Berar subah seat; Gawilgarh nearby.','deccan',
    { old:'Achalpur (ancient) / Ellichpur → Achalpur',
      more:'Capital of Berar under the Bahmanis and then of the Imad Shahi dynasty, founded by Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk (c. 1490). Ahmadnagar absorbed Berar in 1574, and Akbar took it in 1596 after Chand Bibi ceded it. Ellichpur became the seat of the Mughal subah of Berar. Gawilgarh hill fort nearby was stormed by Arthur Wellesley in 1803.' }]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-vijayanagara', group:'his-med', title:'Vijayanagara & South Indian States (1336–1700)', bn:'বিজয়নগর ও দক্ষিণের রাজ্য', icon:'🐘', world:false,
 intro:'হাম্পি → পেনুকোন্ডা → চন্দ্রগিরি → ভেলোর: তালিকোটার (১৫৬৫) পর রাজধানী দক্ষিণে সরল; রায়চুর দোয়াবের লড়াই আর নায়ক রাজ্যগুলোর উত্থান মানচিত্রে দেখো।',
 types:{cap:['Vijayanagara capital / seat','#c62828'], battle:['Battle / captured fort','#6d4c41'], nayak:['Nayaka & successor states','#1565c0'], port:['Temple, matha & port','#2e7d32']},
 pts:[
  ['Vijayanagara (Hampi)',15.335,76.462,'Karnataka (Vijayanagara)','Founded 1336 by Harihara & Bukka (Sangama) on Tungabhadra; Vitthala, Virupaksha; visitors Conti, Abdur Razzaq, Paes, Nuniz; UNESCO.','cap',
    { old:'Kishkindha / Pampa-kshetra (epic); Vijayanagara / Vidyanagara ("city of victory/learning") → Hampi',
      more:'Founded in 1336 by Harihara I and Bukka I of the Sangama dynasty, traditionally with the blessing of sage Vidyaranya. Four dynasties ruled: Sangama, Saluva, Tuluva (Krishnadevaraya, 1509–29, author of Amuktamalyada) and Aravidu. Foreign visitors: Nicolo de Conti, Abdur Razzaq, Domingo Paes, Fernao Nuniz. Vitthala temple stone chariot, Virupaksha and Lotus Mahal survive. Sacked after Talikota (1565); UNESCO 1986.' }],
  ['Kampili — precursor kingdom',15.407,76.610,'Karnataka (Ballari)','Kampili kingdom crushed by Muhammad bin Tughlaq (1327) for sheltering Gurshasp; Harihara & Bukka served here.','cap',
    { old:'Kampili / Kummata (fort of the Kampili kingdom)',
      more:'Small kingdom of Kampiladeva on the Tungabhadra. When it sheltered Bahauddin Gurshasp, a rebel cousin of Muhammad bin Tughlaq, the Sultan crushed it (c. 1327). By tradition Harihara and Bukka served Kampili, were taken to Delhi, and were later sent back to govern the region — they then founded Vijayanagara (1336).' }],
  ['Penukonda — post-1565 capital',14.085,77.597,'Andhra Pradesh (Sri Sathya Sai)','Aravidu Tirumala shifted capital here after Talikota (1565); Gagan Mahal; fortified hill.','cap',
    { old:'Ghanagiri / Penugonda ("big hill")',
      more:'After Talikota (1565), Tirumala Deva Raya of the Aravidu dynasty moved the court here from ruined Hampi. The hill fort has the Gagan Mahal palace, Babayya dargah and Jain and Hindu temples. Venkatapati Raya (Venkata II) later shifted to Chandragiri. A Telugu inscription and the Penukonda fort walls survive.' }],
  ['Chandragiri — Aravidu seat',13.583,79.317,'Andhra Pradesh (Tirupati)','Later Vijayanagara seat (Venkata II era); Raja Mahal; 1639 Madras grant to Francis Day confirmed from Chandragiri.','cap',
    { old:'Chandragiri ("moon hill") — Yadava Narayanavanam chiefs, then Vijayanagara',
      more:'Fort taken by Saluva Narasimha and later a Vijayanagara capital under Venkata II (Venkatapati Raya, 1585–1614) and his successors. In 1639 Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka granted Madraspatnam to Francis Day of the English East India Company; the grant was confirmed by Peda Venkata Raya from Chandragiri — the origin of Fort St George, Madras. Raja Mahal is now a museum.' }],
  ['Vellore Fort',12.921,79.128,'Tamil Nadu','Aravidu capital under Venkata II (early 17th c.); Jalakanteswarar temple; later Vellore Mutiny (1806).','cap',
    { old:'Vellore (Rayavellore under Vijayanagara)',
      more:'Granite fort with moat built by Vijayanagara chieftains (Chinna Bommi Nayak, 16th c.). Venkata II made Vellore an Aravidu capital in the early 17th century. It later passed to Bijapur, the Marathas and the Carnatic Nawabs. Tipu Sultan\'s family was held here after 1799, and the Vellore Mutiny (10 July 1806) broke out here against new dress rules.' }],
  ['Raichur (1520)',16.200,77.370,'Karnataka','Krishnadevaraya defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur (1520) and took Raichur fort; Raichur doab rivalry.','battle',
    { old:'Raichur (Kakatiya fort, 1294)',
      more:'Raichur Doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra was fought over by Vijayanagara and the Bahmanis/Bijapur for two centuries. In May 1520 Krishnadevaraya decisively defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur and captured Raichur fort, with help of Portuguese musketeers (Nuniz). The fort\'s Kakatiya inscription of Gore Gangaya Reddy dates to 1294.' }],
  ['Mudgal — Raichur doab fort',16.020,76.430,'Karnataka (Raichur)','Bitterly contested Vijayanagara–Bahmani fort; 1366 war of Bukka I & Muhammad Shah I Bahmani.','battle',
    { old:'Mudgal / Mudugal (Yadava–Kakatiya fort)',
      more:'Strategic doab fort contested between Vijayanagara and the Bahmanis. In 1366 Bukka I took Mudgal and killed its garrison; Muhammad Shah I Bahmani retaliated in a war reported by Ferishta as the first use of gunpowder artillery in the Deccan. The fort with a double wall and moat changed hands several times and later passed to Bijapur.' }],
  ['Udayagiri (1513) — Gajapati war',14.867,79.317,'Andhra Pradesh (Nellore)','Krishnadevaraya took this Gajapati fort (1513–14) opening his Kalinga campaign against Prataparudra.','battle',
    { old:'Udayagiri (Gajapati fort, Nellore)',
      more:'Strong hill fort held by the Gajapatis of Odisha. Krishnadevaraya besieged and captured it in 1513–14, opening his Kalinga campaign against Gajapati Prataparudra; he brought an image of Bala Krishna from here to Hampi (Krishna temple, 1515). The campaign ended with peace and his marriage to Prataparudra\'s daughter.' }],
  ['Kondavidu (1515)',16.260,80.265,'Andhra Pradesh (Palnadu)','Reddi then Gajapati hill fort captured by Krishnadevaraya (1515); later Qutb Shahi.','battle',
    { old:'Kondaveedu (Reddi capital)',
      more:'Capital of the Reddi kingdom (14th–15th c.), later a Gajapati stronghold. Krishnadevaraya captured it in 1515 during his Kalinga campaign, taking Prataparudra\'s son prisoner. Later held by the Qutb Shahis of Golconda, and then by the French and the British. Ruined fort walls, granaries and temples survive in Palnadu district.' }],
  ['Madurai Nayaks — Tirumala Nayak palace',9.915,78.124,'Tamil Nadu (Madurai)','Vishwanatha Nayaka (1529) began Madurai Nayaks; palace of Tirumala Nayak (1636); Kumara Kampana\'s campaign (c. 1371) ended Madurai Sultanate.','nayak',
    { old:'Madurai (Madura); palace built as Tirumala Nayakkar Mahal',
      more:'Kumara Kampana, son of Bukka I, ended the Madurai Sultanate (c. 1371), as described by his wife Gangadevi in Madhura Vijayam. Vishwanatha Nayaka (1529) founded the Madurai Nayak dynasty and the palayakkarar (poligar) system. Tirumala Nayak (1623–59) built the Indo-Saracenic style palace (1636) and expanded the Meenakshi temple. Rani Mangammal later ruled as regent.' }],
  ['Tiruchirappalli — Rock Fort',10.828,78.697,'Tamil Nadu','Madurai Nayak capital at times (1616–34, 1665–1736); Rani Mangammal; later Carnatic Wars siege site.','nayak',
    { old:'Tirisirapuram / Uraiyur (Chola capital nearby); Trichinopoly (British)',
      more:'The 83 m Rock Fort has Pallava cave temples (Mahendravarman I) and the Ucchi Pillayar temple on top. The Madurai Nayaks used Tiruchirappalli as capital at times (1616–34, 1665–1736), including under Rani Mangammal. During the Carnatic Wars it was besieged (1751–52) when British-backed Muhammad Ali held out against Chanda Sahib and the French.' }],
  ['Thanjavur Nayaks & Marathas — palace',10.791,79.136,'Tamil Nadu (Thanjavur)','Sevappa Nayak (16th c.); Raghunatha Nayak; Venkoji (Ekoji) set up Maratha rule 1674; Saraswati Mahal library.','nayak',
    { old:'Tanjore (British); Thanjavur Nayak and Maratha palace',
      more:'Sevappa Nayak founded the Thanjavur Nayak dynasty (c. 1532) under Vijayanagara; Raghunatha Nayak was a great patron of Carnatic music. Venkoji (Ekoji), half-brother of Shivaji, set up the Thanjavur Maratha dynasty in 1674–76. Serfoji II enriched the Saraswati Mahal library, one of Asia\'s oldest. Thanjavur painting and Tanjore dolls developed here.' }],
  ['Gingee (Senji) Fort',12.251,79.396,'Tamil Nadu (Viluppuram)','Gingee Nayaks; Shivaji took it 1677; Rajaram held out against Zulfiqar Khan (1690–98); "Troy of the East".','nayak',
    { old:'Senji / Chenji (Tamil); Gingee (European)',
      more:'Three-hill fort (Rajagiri, Krishnagiri, Chandrayandurg) of the Gingee Nayaks. Shivaji took it in 1677 during his Karnataka campaign. Rajaram, Shivaji\'s younger son, escaped here and held out against Zulfiqar Khan\'s Mughal siege for about eight years (1690–98). The British called it the "Troy of the East". Later held by the French (1750–61).' }],
  ['Keladi — Nayaka origin',14.224,75.016,'Karnataka (Shivamogga)','Keladi Nayakas (Chaudappa, 1499) rose as Vijayanagara feudatories; Rameshwara–Veerabhadra temple.','nayak',
    { old:'Keladi (first seat of the Keladi / Ikkeri Nayakas)',
      more:'Chaudappa Nayaka (c. 1499) founded the Keladi Nayaka line as feudatories of Vijayanagara. They ruled the Malnad and coastal Karnataka (Kanara) and controlled pepper and rice trade with the Portuguese. The Rameshwara and Veerabhadra temples (16th c.) show a mix of Hoysala, Vijayanagara and Kadamba styles. Capital moved to Ikkeri and later Bidanur.' }],
  ['Ikkeri — Keladi capital',14.135,75.023,'Karnataka (Shivamogga)','Capital of Keladi Nayakas (16th–17th c.) before Bidanur; Aghoreshwara temple; Keladi Chennamma fought Aurangzeb\'s forces.','nayak',
    { old:'Ikkeri (Keladi capital c. 1560–1640) → capital moved to Bidanur (Nagar)',
      more:'Capital of the Keladi Nayakas after Keladi; the Aghoreshwara temple is a large granite structure. The "Ikkeri pagoda" coin was a regional currency. Keladi Chennamma (ruled 1672–97) gave shelter to Rajaram fleeing to Gingee and fought Aurangzeb\'s forces. Hyder Ali annexed Bidanur in 1763.' }],
  ['Mysore — Wodeyars',12.304,76.654,'Karnataka (Mysuru)','Wodeyars, Vijayanagara feudatories; Raja Wodeyar took Srirangapatna 1610 — origin of Mysore Dasara.','nayak',
    { old:'Mahishuru / Mahishasura-uru (legend) → Mysore → Mysuru (2014)',
      more:'The Wodeyars began as Vijayanagara feudatories (Yaduraya, 1399, trad.). Raja Wodeyar I seized Srirangapatna from the Vijayanagara viceroy in 1610 and began the Mysore Dasara celebrations modelled on Hampi\'s Mahanavami. Chikka Devaraja expanded the state. Hyder Ali took real power in 1761; the British restored the Wodeyars in 1799.' }],
  ['Chitradurga Fort',14.215,76.395,'Karnataka','Nayakas of Chitradurga (Madakari Nayaka); seven-ring fort; Onake Obavva legend; taken by Hyder Ali 1779.','nayak',
    { old:'Chitrakaladurga ("picturesque fort"); Sulebail; Chitaldoorg (British)',
      more:'Seven-ring stone fort (Elusuttina Kote) built mainly by the Nayakas of Chitradurga, Vijayanagara feudatories who became independent after 1565. Legend tells of Onake Obavva, who killed Hyder Ali\'s soldiers with a pestle (onake) at a crevice. Hyder Ali captured it from Madakari Nayaka V in 1779. It has ingenious rain-water tanks.' }],
  ['Ramanathapuram — Sethupathis',9.383,78.833,'Tamil Nadu','Sethupathi chiefs, guardians of Rameswaram route, autonomous from Madurai Nayaks (17th c.); Ramalinga Vilasam palace.','nayak',
    { old:'Ramnad (British); Mugavai (Tamil); Maravar country',
      more:'The Sethupathis ("lords of the causeway") were Maravar chiefs appointed to protect pilgrims to Rameswaram. Under Raghunatha Kilavan (late 17th c.) they became nearly independent of the Madurai Nayaks. Their Ramalinga Vilasam palace has fine murals. Bhaskara Sethupathi later sponsored Swami Vivekananda\'s trip to the Chicago Parliament (1893).' }],
  ['Bhatkal — horse-import port',13.967,74.567,'Karnataka (Uttara Kannada)','Vijayanagara port for Arab & Persian horses; Krishnadevaraya bought Portuguese horses via Bhatkal/Goa.','port',
    { old:'Bhatkal / Batecala (Portuguese)',
      more:'Major Vijayanagara port on the Kanara coast where Arab and Persian horses were imported — vital because South India could not breed good war horses. The Portuguese tried to control the horse trade via Goa (taken 1510); Krishnadevaraya bargained with Albuquerque for horses. Bhatkal later came under the Keladi Nayakas; Jain basadis and old mosques survive.' }],
  ['Calicut (Kozhikode) — Zamorin',11.248,75.780,'Kerala','Zamorin\'s port; Zheng He & Abdur Razzaq (1442) visited; Vasco da Gama landed at Kappad 1498; Kunjali Marakkars.','port',
    { old:'Kozhikode → Calicut (European); "City of Spices"',
      more:'Port of the Zamorin (Samoothiri) rulers. Chinese admiral Zheng He visited (1405–33) and Persian envoy Abdur Razzaq came in 1442. Vasco da Gama landed at Kappad near Calicut on 20 May 1498, opening the sea route to Europe. The Kunjali Marakkars led the Zamorin\'s navy against the Portuguese. The word "calico" (cotton cloth) comes from Calicut. UNESCO City of Literature (2023).' }],
  ['Tirumala — Venkateswara',13.683,79.347,'Andhra Pradesh (Tirupati)','Krishnadevaraya made rich gifts (statues with queens in temple); Annamacharya\'s kirtanas; Saluva & Aravidu patronage.','port',
    { old:'Tiruvengadam / Venkatachalam (Tamil–Sanskrit); Seven Hills',
      more:'Ancient Vaishnava shrine praised by the Alvars. Saluva Narasimha and the Tuluva and Aravidu kings were major patrons; Krishnadevaraya visited several times, and copper statues of him with queens Chinna Devi and Tirumala Devi stand in the temple. Annamacharya (15th c.) composed thousands of sankirtanas to Venkateswara. Tirupati laddu has a GI tag.' }],
  ['Lepakshi — Veerabhadra temple',13.804,77.610,'Andhra Pradesh (Sri Sathya Sai)','Built by Virupanna & Viranna (c. 1530s, Achyuta Raya); finest Vijayanagara murals; hanging pillar; monolithic Nandi.','port',
    { old:'Lepakshi ("rise, bird" — Ramayana legend of Jatayu)',
      more:'Veerabhadra temple built c. 1530s under Achyuta Deva Raya by brothers Virupanna and Viranna, governors of Penukonda. Its ceilings have the finest surviving Vijayanagara murals, including a large Veerabhadra painting. It has a "hanging pillar" that does not touch the floor, a seven-hooded Naga linga and a huge monolithic Nandi nearby. On UNESCO\'s tentative list.' }],
  ['Sringeri — Vidyaranya',13.420,75.250,'Karnataka (Chikkamagaluru)','Sharada Peetha of Shankara; Vidyaranya (Madhava), guru of Harihara & Bukka; Vidyashankara temple (1338).','port',
    { old:'Rishyashringa-giri (traditional, after sage Rishyashringa)',
      more:'Seat of the Dakshinamnaya Sharada Peetha, traditionally founded by Adi Shankaracharya. Vidyaranya (Madhavacharya), a pontiff here, is credited with guiding Harihara and Bukka to found Vijayanagara (1336); his brother Sayana wrote commentaries on the Vedas. The Vidyashankara temple (1338) has 12 zodiac pillars aligned with the sun. On the Tunga river.' }]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-mughal', group:'his-med', title:'Mughal Empire (1526–1757)', bn:'মুঘল সাম্রাজ্য', icon:'🏰', world:false,
 intro:'যুদ্ধ (পানিপথ → খানুয়া → ঘাঘরা → চৌসা → কনৌজ), রাজধানী (আগ্রা, ফতেপুর সিক্রি, লাহোর, শাহজাহানাবাদ) আর আকবরের সুবা-রাজধানী একসঙ্গে দেখো।',
 types:{cap:['Capital city','#c62828'], battle:['Battle','#6d4c41'], subah:['Subah capital / province','#1565c0'], mon:['Monument site','#8e24aa']},
 pts:[
  ['Panipat (1526, 1556, 1761)',29.402,76.973,'Haryana','1526 Babur bt Ibrahim Lodi (tulughma, artillery); 1556 Bairam Khan/Akbar bt Hemu; 1761 Abdali bt Marathas (Sadashivrao Bhau).','battle',
    { old:'Panduprastha (one of the five villages of Mahabharata legend) → Panipat',
      more:'First Battle (21 April 1526): Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi using the tulughma flanking tactic and artillery (Ustad Ali, Mustafa) — start of Mughal rule. Second Battle (5 Nov 1556): Bairam Khan for young Akbar defeated Hemu (Vikramaditya), who was struck in the eye by an arrow. Third Battle (14 Jan 1761): Ahmad Shah Abdali crushed the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau; Vishwasrao died. Kala Amb marks the 1761 site.' }],
  ['Khanwa (1527)',27.035,77.543,'Rajasthan (Bharatpur)','Babur defeated Rana Sanga\'s Rajput confederacy; Babur took title Ghazi.','battle',
    { old:'Khanua / Khanwa (near Fatehpur Sikri, Bharatpur)',
      more:'On 17 March 1527 Babur defeated the Rajput confederacy of Rana Sanga of Mewar, joined by Hasan Khan Mewati and Mahmud Lodi. Before the battle Babur gave up wine and declared the war a jihad, taking the title Ghazi afterwards. Victory secured Mughal rule in North India; Sanga died in 1528. Described in the Baburnama.' }],
  ['Chanderi (1528)',24.720,78.130,'Madhya Pradesh (Ashoknagar)','Babur stormed Medini Rai\'s fort (1528); earlier a Malwa Sultanate stronghold; famed Chanderi weave.','battle',
    { old:'Chandrapuri / Chedi (trad.); Chanderi',
      more:'In January 1528 Babur stormed the fort of Medini Rai, an ally of Rana Sanga, after which the Rajput defenders performed jauhar. Chanderi had been a Pratihara town and a stronghold of the Malwa Sultanate; the Koshak Mahal and Badal Mahal gate survive. Chanderi saris (silk–cotton) have a GI tag.' }],
  ['Ghaghra (1529)',25.800,84.800,'Bihar (Saran)','Babur defeated the Afghans of Bihar & Bengal (Mahmud Lodi, Nusrat Shah) at Ghaghra–Ganga confluence.','battle',
    { old:'Battle of the Gogra (Ghaghra–Ganga confluence)',
      more:'On 6 May 1529 Babur defeated the Afghan chiefs of Bihar under Mahmud Lodi, backed by Nusrat Shah of Bengal, near the confluence of the Ghaghra and Ganga. It was one of the first major river battles in Indian history and Babur\'s last great campaign; he died in Agra in December 1530.' }],
  ['Chausa (1539)',25.508,83.882,'Bihar (Buxar)','Sher Khan (Sher Shah) routed Humayun; Humayun crossed the Ganga with a water-carrier\'s help.','battle',
    { old:'Chausa (on the Karmanasa–Ganga, Buxar)',
      more:'On 26 June 1539 Sher Khan surprised Humayun\'s camp at dawn and routed his army. Humayun escaped across the Ganga with the help of a water-carrier (bhishti) Nizam, whom he later let rule for a day (trad.). Sher Khan then crowned himself Sher Shah. Chausa is also known for the Chausa mango variety.' }],
  ['Kannauj / Bilgram (1540)',27.180,80.030,'Uttar Pradesh (Kannauj)','Sher Shah decisively defeated Humayun, who fled to Persia — Sur rule (1540–55).','battle',
    { old:'Battle of Bilgram (Kanyakubja / Kannauj)',
      more:'On 17 May 1540 Sher Shah decisively defeated Humayun on the Ganga near Kannauj. Humayun fled through Sindh to Persia, where Shah Tahmasp gave him refuge. Sher Shah founded the Sur dynasty (1540–55): rupiya coin, Grand Trunk Road, sarais, dak chowki, and land measurement reforms later used by Akbar\'s Todar Mal.' }],
  ['Machhiwara (1555)',30.910,76.200,'Punjab (Ludhiana)','Humayun\'s army (Bairam Khan) defeated Afghan Tatar Khan on the Sutlej during his return.','battle',
    { old:'Machhiwara (on the Sutlej, Ludhiana)',
      more:'On 15 May 1555, during Humayun\'s return from exile, Bairam Khan defeated the Afghan general Tatar Khan and Haibat Khan of the Sur army across the Sutlej. This opened the Punjab to the Mughals. Machhiwara is also linked to Guru Gobind Singh\'s escape after Chamkaur (1704).' }],
  ['Sirhind (1555)',30.625,76.386,'Punjab (Fatehgarh Sahib)','Humayun defeated Sikandar Shah Sur and regained Delhi.','battle',
    { old:'Sirhind / Sahrind (Persian, "head of Hind")',
      more:'On 22 June 1555 Humayun\'s forces (with Bairam Khan and young Akbar) defeated Sikandar Shah Sur, ending Sur rule; Humayun reoccupied Delhi in July 1555 but died in January 1556 after falling down the stairs of his library (Sher Mandal). Sirhind was later the seat of the Mughal faujdar Wazir Khan, who was killed by Banda Bahadur in 1710.' }],
  ['Haldighati (1576)',24.892,73.698,'Rajasthan (Rajsamand)','Maharana Pratap vs Akbar\'s army under Man Singh; Pratap escaped on Chetak; Badauni was an eyewitness.','battle',
    { old:'Haldighati ("turmeric-coloured pass"); Khamnor / Rakt Talai',
      more:'On 18 June 1576 Maharana Pratap of Mewar fought Akbar\'s army led by Raja Man Singh of Amber and Asaf Khan. Hakim Khan Sur fought for Pratap and Bhil archers under Rana Punja supported him. Pratap escaped on his horse Chetak, which died after the battle. Badauni fought on the Mughal side and wrote an eyewitness account. Pratap later regained most of Mewar.' }],
  ['Dharmat (1658)',23.033,75.667,'Madhya Pradesh (Ujjain)','Near Fatehabad on the Gambhir — Aurangzeb & Murad defeated Jaswant Singh (for Dara) in the war of succession.','battle',
    { old:'Dharmatpur near Fatehabad (Ujjain)',
      more:'On 15 April 1658, in the war of succession among Shah Jahan\'s sons, Aurangzeb and Murad Bakhsh defeated the imperial army of Maharaja Jaswant Singh of Marwar, who fought for Dara Shikoh. Aurangzeb named the site Fatehabad. It was Aurangzeb\'s first victory on his march to Agra.' }],
  ['Samugarh (Fatehabad) (1658)',27.100,78.183,'Uttar Pradesh (Agra)','Aurangzeb defeated Dara Shikoh east of Agra; town renamed Fatehabad; Shah Jahan then confined in Agra Fort.','battle',
    { old:'Samugarh → Fatehabad (renamed by Aurangzeb, 1658)',
      more:'On 29 May 1658 Aurangzeb and Murad defeated Dara Shikoh about 15 km east of Agra; Dara\'s descent from his elephant caused his army to panic. Aurangzeb then besieged Agra Fort, cut off its water and confined Shah Jahan there until his death (1666). Murad was later imprisoned and executed; Aurangzeb crowned himself at Delhi.' }],
  ['Khajwa (1659)',26.052,80.524,'Uttar Pradesh (Fatehpur)','Aurangzeb defeated brother Shah Shuja.','battle',
    { old:'Khajuha / Khajwa (Fatehpur district)',
      more:'On 5 January 1659 Aurangzeb defeated his brother Shah Shuja, governor of Bengal, in the war of succession. Mir Jumla then pursued Shuja into Bengal and Arakan, where Shuja disappeared (1661). Aurangzeb built a sarai and garden at Khajuha to mark the victory.' }],
  ['Karnal (1739)',29.725,77.070,'Haryana','Nadir Shah defeated Muhammad Shah; sack of Delhi, Peacock Throne & Koh-i-Noor taken.','battle',
    { old:'Karnal (traditionally after Karna of the Mahabharata)',
      more:'On 24 February 1739 Nadir Shah of Persia defeated the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah\'s large army in about three hours. Saadat Khan of Awadh was captured. In Delhi Nadir Shah ordered a massacre (March 1739) and carried off the Peacock Throne and the Koh-i-Noor, and took the lands west of the Indus. It exposed Mughal weakness.' }],
  ['Agra — Fort & Taj Mahal',27.180,78.021,'Uttar Pradesh','Sikandar Lodi\'s city (1504); Akbar\'s red-sandstone fort (1565); Taj Mahal 2.5 km SE (Shah Jahan); both UNESCO.','cap',
    { old:'Agra (Sikandar Lodi, 1504) → Akbarabad (Mughal name, from Shah Jahan\'s time); Badalgarh (earlier fort)',
      more:'Sikandar Lodi made Agra his capital (1504). Akbar rebuilt the old Badalgarh fort in red sandstone (1565–73). Shah Jahan added marble palaces (Khas Mahal, Musamman Burj), where Aurangzeb confined him until 1666. The Taj Mahal (c. 1632–53), tomb of Mumtaz Mahal by architect Ustad Ahmad Lahauri, stands 2.5 km downstream. Both UNESCO 1983; Agra was the main capital until Shahjahanabad (1648).' }],
  ['Fatehpur Sikri',27.091,77.661,'Uttar Pradesh (Agra)','Akbar\'s capital 1571–85; Buland Darwaza (Gujarat victory); Ibadat Khana debates 1575; Panch Mahal; UNESCO.','cap',
    { old:'Sikri (village) → Fathabad / Fatehpur ("city of victory", 1573 after Gujarat conquest)',
      more:'Akbar built his capital (1571–85) at Sikri, home of Sufi Shaikh Salim Chishti, whose blessing he credited for the birth of Salim (Jahangir). The Buland Darwaza (1575–76) marks the conquest of Gujarat. The Ibadat Khana (1575) hosted religious debates leading to Sulh-i-Kul and Din-i-Ilahi (1582). Diwan-i-Khas, Panch Mahal and Jodha Bai palace survive. Abandoned c. 1585, partly for water shortage. UNESCO 1986.' }],
  ['Delhi — Shahjahanabad (Red Fort)',28.656,77.240,'Delhi','Shah Jahan\'s capital (1648); Diwan-i-Khas with Peacock Throne; Jama Masjid; UNESCO.','cap',
    { old:'Shahjahanabad (Shah Jahan, 1648); fort called Qila-i-Mubarak; Old Delhi',
      more:'Shah Jahan moved the capital from Agra and built the walled city of Shahjahanabad (1639–48), the seventh city of Delhi, with the Qila-i-Mubarak (Red Fort) by architect Ahmad Lahauri. The Diwan-i-Khas held the Peacock Throne, taken by Nadir Shah in 1739. The Jama Masjid and Chandni Chowk (laid out by Jahanara) followed. Bahadur Shah Zafar was tried here (1858); the PM hoists the flag on 15 August. UNESCO 2007.' }],
  ['Lahore Fort',31.590,74.310,'Pakistan','Mughal capital 1585–98 under Akbar; Sheesh Mahal (Shah Jahan); Badshahi mosque (Aurangzeb); Lahore subah.','cap',
    { old:'Lahore (Lahaur, Lohkot in tradition); fort called Shahi Qila',
      more:'Akbar made Lahore his capital from 1585 to 1598 while watching the north-west frontier, and rebuilt the fort in brick. Jahangir and Shah Jahan added palaces; Shah Jahan\'s Sheesh Mahal and Naulakha pavilion are famous, and Aurangzeb built the Badshahi Mosque (1673) opposite. Later Ranjit Singh\'s capital. With the Shalimar Gardens, UNESCO 1981.' }],
  ['Kabul — Bagh-e Babur',34.503,69.160,'Afghanistan','Babur\'s base before India (1504) and his burial place; Kabul subah capital.','cap',
    { old:'Kabura / Kubha (ancient); Kabul',
      more:'Babur took Kabul in 1504 and ruled from here before invading India. He asked to be buried in Kabul; his body was moved from Agra to the terraced garden now called Bagh-e Babur (c. 1540s). Shah Jahan added a marble mosque (1647). Kabul remained a Mughal subah; its loss and the Afghan frontier were constant concerns.' }],
  ['Sikandra — Akbar\'s tomb',27.221,77.951,'Uttar Pradesh (Agra)','Akbar\'s tomb, completed by Jahangir (1613); five-storeyed with marble top storey and inlay gateway.','mon',
    { old:'Sikandra (named after Sikandar Lodi)',
      more:'Akbar planned his own tomb; Jahangir completed it in 1613. It has five storeys, the first four in red sandstone and the top a white-marble open court with a cenotaph, showing a blend of Hindu, Buddhist, Jain and Islamic elements. The southern gateway with inlay work and four minarets prefigures the Taj. Jat raiders under Raja Ram plundered it in 1688.' }],
  ['Humayun\'s Tomb & Purana Qila',28.593,77.251,'Delhi','Tomb by Haji Begum (1570), first garden-tomb, Persian architect Mirak Mirza Ghiyas; Purana Qila (Dinpanah/Shergarh) 2 km N; UNESCO.','mon',
    { old:'Dinpanah (Humayun, 1533) → Shergarh (Sher Shah); Indraprastha (tradition at Purana Qila)',
      more:'Humayun\'s tomb (1565–72) was built by his widow Haji (Bega) Begum and designed by the Persian architect Mirak Mirza Ghiyas; it is the first garden-tomb with a char-bagh in India and a model for the Taj. It is called the "dormitory of the Mughals". Purana Qila, nearby, was Humayun\'s Dinpanah, rebuilt by Sher Shah (Qila-i-Kuhna mosque, Sher Mandal). UNESCO 1993.' }],
  ['Shahdara — Jahangir\'s tomb',31.622,74.303,'Pakistan','Jahangir\'s tomb (1637) built by Shah Jahan/Nur Jahan across the Ravi from Lahore; Nur Jahan buried nearby.','mon',
    { old:'Shahdara Bagh (Dilkusha garden of Nur Jahan)',
      more:'Jahangir died in 1627 near Bhimber; he was buried at Shahdara, across the Ravi from Lahore, in the Dilkusha garden. Shah Jahan built the tomb (completed 1637), with four tall minarets and pietra dura inlay but no dome. Nur Jahan\'s tomb and Asaf Khan\'s tomb are nearby.' }],
  ['Srinagar — Shalimar Bagh',34.150,74.873,'Jammu & Kashmir','Mughal terraced char-bagh by Jahangir for Nur Jahan (1619); Nishat Bagh (Asaf Khan) nearby on Dal lake.','mon',
    { old:'Farah Bakhsh ("delightful"); Faiz Bakhsh (upper terrace added by Shah Jahan)',
      more:'Jahangir laid out Shalimar Bagh for Nur Jahan in 1619 as a terraced char-bagh fed by a canal from the hills, on the banks of Dal Lake. Shah Jahan extended it (1630). Nearby Nishat Bagh (1633) was built by Asaf Khan, Nur Jahan\'s brother. Jahangir called Kashmir a paradise on earth. Mughal Gardens of Kashmir are on UNESCO\'s tentative list.' }],
  ['Aurangabad — Bibi ka Maqbara',19.901,75.321,'Maharashtra (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar)','Tomb of Aurangzeb\'s wife Dilras Banu (1660) by Azam Shah — "Taj of the Deccan"; Aurangabad was Mughal Deccan HQ.','mon',
    { old:'Khadki (Malik Ambar, 1610) → Fatehnagar → Aurangabad (1653) → Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar (2023)',
      more:'Malik Ambar founded Khadki in 1610; it became Aurangabad when Aurangzeb was Deccan viceroy, and later Mughal Deccan headquarters. Azam Shah built the Bibi ka Maqbara (1660s–78) for his mother Dilras Banu Begum, designed by Ata-ullah; it imitates the Taj Mahal and is called the "Taj of the Deccan". Aurangzeb died at Ahmednagar (1707) and is buried at Khuldabad nearby.' }],
  ['Asirgarh Fort',21.471,76.294,'Madhya Pradesh (Burhanpur)','"Key to the Deccan"; Akbar took it from Faruqi Bahadur Shah (1601) — his last conquest.','mon',
    { old:'Asirgarh (traditionally from Asa Ahir, a herdsman chief)',
      more:'Hill fort in the Satpuras, called the "key to the Deccan" (Dakhin ka darwaza) since it controlled the route from north India to the Deccan. Akbar besieged it and took it from Bahadur Shah Faruqi of Khandesh on 17 January 1601, reportedly by bribery and blockade — his last conquest. Later held by Marathas (Scindia) and the British.' }],
  ['Kalanaur — Akbar\'s coronation',32.0100,75.0900,'Punjab (Gurdaspur)','Akbar crowned on a brick platform (Takht-i-Akbari) here in Feb 1556 after Humayun\'s death.','mon',
    { old:'Kalanaur (Gurdaspur); platform called Takht-i-Akbari',
      more:'When Humayun died in January 1556, 13-year-old Akbar was campaigning in Punjab with Bairam Khan. He was crowned on 14 February 1556 on a brick platform in a garden at Kalanaur, now known as Takht-i-Akbari (ASI protected). Bairam Khan became regent (vakil) and soon won the Second Battle of Panipat.' }],
  ['Umarkot — Akbar\'s birthplace',25.361,69.736,'Pakistan','Akbar born here 1542 in the Rajput fort of Rana Prasad while Humayun was in exile.','mon',
    { old:'Amarkot (Sodha Rajput fort of Rana Prasad) → Umerkot',
      more:'During his exile Humayun was given shelter by Rana Prasad, the Sodha Rajput ruler of Amarkot in the Thar desert. Akbar was born here on 15 October 1542 to Hamida Banu Begum. A memorial marks the site. Umerkot is in Sindh, Pakistan, and still has a large Hindu population.' }],
  ['Kandahar',31.621,65.716,'Afghanistan','Contested with Safavid Persia; lost by Shah Jahan 1649 — three failed sieges (1649–53).','subah',
    { old:'Alexandria Arachosia (Greek, trad.); Qandahar',
      more:'Gateway between India, Persia and Central Asia, and long contested between the Mughals and Safavid Persia. Akbar took it in 1595; the Persians took it in 1622 under Jahangir. Shah Jahan regained it in 1638 but lost it in 1649 to Shah Abbas II; three Mughal sieges under Aurangzeb and Dara (1649–53) failed. Ashokan bilingual (Greek–Aramaic) edicts were found here.' }],
  ['Multan — Mughal subah',30.198,71.471,'Pakistan','One of Akbar\'s 12 subahs (1580); also a Sufi centre (Suhrawardi order).','subah',
    { old:'Mulasthana / Kashyapapura (ancient); "City of Saints"',
      more:'Ancient sun-temple city conquered by Muhammad bin Qasim (713). Under Akbar it became one of the original 12 subahs (1580). It was a centre of the Suhrawardi Sufi order (Bahauddin Zakariya, Shah Rukn-e-Alam\'s tomb). Langah sultans ruled before the Mughals. Ranjit Singh took it in 1818; the Second Anglo-Sikh War began here (1848).' }],
  ['Thatta — Sindh (Thatta) subah',24.747,67.928,'Pakistan','Seat of Thatta subah after Akbar annexed Sindh (1591); Shah Jahan Mosque (1647); Makli necropolis nearby.','subah',
    { old:'Thatta / Nagar Thatta; Debal region; Pattala (trad. Greek)',
      more:'Capital of the Samma and Arghun/Tarkhan rulers of lower Sindh. Akbar annexed Sindh in 1591 and made Thatta a sarkar of the Multan subah; it later became the seat of the Thatta subah. Shah Jahan built the tiled Shah Jahan Mosque (1647) as thanks for hospitality. The vast Makli necropolis nearby is a UNESCO site (1981).' }],
  ['Ajmer — Akbari Fort (Daulat Khana)',26.461,74.633,'Rajasthan','Akbar\'s fort-palace in Ajmer subah; Jahangir received Sir Thomas Roe here (1616).','subah',
    { old:'Ajayameru (Chauhan) → Ajmer; fort called Daulat Khana / Magazine',
      more:'Akbar built the fort-palace (1570) as a residence on his pilgrimages to Moinuddin Chishti\'s dargah and as a base for Rajputana campaigns; Ajmer became one of the 12 subahs. Jahangir lived here 1613–16 and received Sir Thomas Roe, the English ambassador of James I, in 1616. Later used by the British as an arsenal (Magazine); now a museum.' }],
  ['Allahabad Fort',25.430,81.877,'Uttar Pradesh (Prayagraj)','Akbar built the fort (1583) at the Sangam and renamed city Ilahabas; Allahabad subah; Salim rebelled here (1600).','subah',
    { old:'Prayag → Ilahabas (Akbar, 1583) → Ilahabad / Allahabad (Shah Jahan) → Prayagraj (2018)',
      more:'Akbar built the fort in 1583 at the Sangam of the Ganga and Yamuna and named the city Ilahabas; it became one of his subahs. Prince Salim (Jahangir) rebelled and set up court here in 1600. The fort holds the Ashokan pillar with Samudragupta\'s Prayag prashasti (Harishena) and Jahangir\'s inscription, and the Akshayavat tree. Kumbh Mela site.' }],
  ['Ayodhya — Awadh subah',26.799,82.204,'Uttar Pradesh (Ayodhya)','Seat of Awadh subah under Akbar; Awadh Nawabs later ruled from Faizabad & Lucknow.','subah',
    { old:'Saketa (Buddhist/Jain texts); Kosala capital; Awadh / Oudh (Mughal & British)',
      more:'Ancient Saketa, capital of Kosala, linked with Rama, and a Jain pilgrimage site (birth of five tirthankaras). Under Akbar it was the seat of the Awadh subah (1580). The Nawabs of Awadh (from 1722) ruled from nearby Faizabad and then Lucknow. Ayodhya was merged with Faizabad as a district renamed Ayodhya in 2018.' }],
  ['Ujjain — Malwa subah',23.183,75.777,'Madhya Pradesh','Capital of Malwa subah after Akbar\'s conquest (1561–62, Baz Bahadur); Jai Singh II built observatory here.','subah',
    { old:'Avantika / Ujjayini (Avanti mahajanapada); Ujjain',
      more:'Capital of ancient Avanti and a Kumbh Mela city with the Mahakaleshwar jyotirlinga. Ancient Indian astronomers took Ujjain as the prime meridian. Akbar\'s army took Malwa from Baz Bahadur (1561–62) and Ujjain became the subah capital. Sawai Jai Singh II, Mughal governor of Malwa, built a Jantar Mantar observatory here (c. 1720s). Later a Scindia capital.' }],
  ['Patna — Bihar subah',25.610,85.141,'Bihar','Sher Shah rebuilt Patna fort (1541); Bihar subah capital; Azim-ush-Shan renamed it Azimabad (1704).','subah',
    { old:'Pataliputra (Maurya) / Kusumapura → Patna (Sher Shah, 1541) → Azimabad (1704)',
      more:'Ancient Pataliputra was the Mauryan and Gupta capital. Sher Shah built a fort here in 1541, reviving Patna as a city. Under the Mughals it was capital of the Bihar subah; Prince Azim-ush-Shan, grandson of Aurangzeb, renamed it Azimabad (1704). Guru Gobind Singh was born here (1666). The English and Dutch had saltpetre factories here.' }],
  ['Rajmahal — Bengal subah (Akbarnagar)',25.050,87.840,'Jharkhand (Sahebganj)','Man Singh made it Bengal capital (1595, Akbarnagar); Daud Khan Karrani killed nearby (1576).','subah',
    { old:'Agmahal → Akbarnagar (Man Singh, 1595) → Rajmahal',
      more:'Akbar\'s forces killed Daud Khan Karrani, last Afghan Sultan of Bengal, at the Battle of Rajmahal (1576). Raja Man Singh, governor of Bengal, made Rajmahal the capital of the Bengal subah in 1595 and named it Akbarnagar. Shah Shuja also ruled from here (1639–59). The Jami Masjid and Sangi Dalan survive; the Rajmahal Hills have Jurassic plant fossils.' }],
  ['Dhaka — Jahangirnagar',23.719,90.388,'Bangladesh','Islam Khan made Dhaka Bengal capital 1610 (Jahangirnagar); Lalbagh Fort (Azam Shah, 1678); later moved to Murshidabad.','subah',
    { old:'Dhakka (pre-Mughal) → Jahangirnagar (Islam Khan, 1610) → Dacca → Dhaka',
      more:'Subahdar Islam Khan Chishti made Dhaka the capital of Bengal in 1610 and named it Jahangirnagar after the emperor, to fight the Baro-Bhuiyans and Arakanese/Portuguese pirates. Shaista Khan built many mosques; Prince Azam Shah began Lalbagh Fort (1678). Dhaka muslin was world-famous. Murshid Quli Khan moved the capital to Murshidabad (c. 1704–17).' }],
  ['Cuttack — Barabati (Orissa subah)',20.484,85.866,'Odisha','Eastern Ganga fort; Orissa annexed by Akbar (1592, Man Singh) and attached to Bengal; later Maratha (Bhonsle).','subah',
    { old:'Kataka ("military camp", Eastern Ganga) → Cuttack',
      more:'Anantavarman Chodaganga made Cuttack the Eastern Ganga capital (12th c.); Barabati fort dates to the 14th century. After Afghan rule, Raja Man Singh annexed Orissa for Akbar in 1592 and it was attached to the Bengal subah. Raghuji Bhonsle\'s Marathas took it in 1751, and the British in 1803. Famous for silver filigree (tarakasi).' }]
 ]
});

GEO.add({
 id:'his-maratha', group:'his-med', title:'Marathas, Sikhs & 18th-century Regional States', bn:'মারাঠা, শিখ ও অষ্টাদশ শতকের আঞ্চলিক রাজ্য', icon:'🚩', world:false,
 intro:'শিবাজির দুর্গ, পেশোয়া যুগের যুদ্ধ, খালসা ও বান্দা বাহাদুর, আর অওধ-হায়দরাবাদ-বাংলা-মাইসোরের উত্তরসূরি রাজ্য — মুঘল পতনের মানচিত্র।',
 types:{mar:['Maratha seat / fort','#ef6c00'], sikh:['Sikh','#1565c0'], reg:['Regional state','#2e7d32'], battle:['Battle / treaty','#c62828']},
 pts:[
  ['Shivneri — Shivaji\'s birthplace',19.197,73.859,'Maharashtra (Pune, Junnar)','Shivaji born here 1630 (19 Feb, per state); hill fort of Shahaji\'s family in Junnar.','mar',
    { old:'Shivneri (named after goddess Shivai); Junnar = Jirnanagara (ancient)',
      more:'Hill fort above Junnar, an ancient trade town with Buddhist caves on the Naneghat route. Shivaji was born here to Jijabai and Shahaji Bhonsle (19 February 1630 per the Maharashtra government; some sources give 1627). The fort has the Shivai Devi temple and Badami tank. It passed to the Mughals and was never held long by Shivaji himself.' }],
  ['Rajgad — first capital',18.246,73.682,'Maharashtra (Pune)','Shivaji\'s capital for c. 25 years before Raigad; "king of forts".','mar',
    { old:'Murumbdev (earlier name of the hill fort) → Rajgad ("king of forts")',
      more:'Shivaji took Murumbdev in the 1640s, rebuilt it and renamed it Rajgad; it served as his capital for about 25 years until he moved to Raigad (1674). His son Rajaram was born here (1670) and his wife Saibai died here (1659). It has three machis (terraces): Padmavati, Suvela and Sanjivani.' }],
  ['Raigad — coronation (1674)',18.235,73.445,'Maharashtra (Raigad)','Shivaji\'s capital; coronation by Gaga Bhatta 6 June 1674 (Chhatrapati); Ashta Pradhan; died here 1680.','mar',
    { old:'Rairi (Chandrarao More fort) → Raigad; "Gibraltar of the East" (Europeans)',
      more:'Shivaji took Rairi from the More family (1656) and made it his capital. On 6 June 1674 he was crowned Chhatrapati here by Gaga Bhatta of Varanasi, starting the Rajyabhisheka Shaka era. His council was the Ashta Pradhan. He died here on 3 April 1680; his samadhi is in the fort. The Mughals captured it in 1689 with Sambhaji\'s family.' }],
  ['Satara (Ajinkyatara)',17.672,73.995,'Maharashtra (Satara)','Capital of Chhatrapati Shahu after 1708 (released by Mughals); real power shifted to the Peshwas.','mar',
    { old:'Sapta-tara (trad., "seven forts/stars"); Ajinkyatara ("invincible star")',
      more:'Shahu, son of Sambhaji, was released by the Mughals after Aurangzeb\'s death (1707), defeated Tarabai at Khed and was crowned at Satara (1708). He appointed Balaji Vishwanath Peshwa (1713), after which real power shifted to the Peshwas at Pune. Ajinkyatara fort overlooks the town. Satara was annexed by Dalhousie under the Doctrine of Lapse (1848).' }],
  ['Pune — Shaniwar Wada',18.519,73.855,'Maharashtra (Pune)','Peshwa seat built by Baji Rao I (1732); Narayanrao murdered here (1773); burnt 1828.','mar',
    { old:'Punyaka / Punawadi (Rashtrakuta copper plate, 8th c.) → Poona → Pune',
      more:'Baji Rao I built the seven-storey Shaniwar Wada palace in 1732, making Pune the seat of the Peshwas. The young Peshwa Narayanrao was murdered here in 1773, leading to the Barabhai council of Nana Phadnavis. After the British took Pune (1817–18) the palace was gutted by a fire in 1828; the Delhi Darwaza survives.' }],
  ['Kolhapur — Tarabai\'s line',16.719,74.233,'Maharashtra (Kolhapur)','Separate Maratha throne of Tarabai/Sambhaji II; Treaty of Warna (1731) with Shahu split the house.','mar',
    { old:'Karvir / Karveer (ancient, Mahalakshmi temple); Kolhapur',
      more:'Tarabai, widow of Rajaram, ruled the Marathas against Aurangzeb (1700–07) in her son Shivaji II\'s name. After Shahu\'s return, a rival line under Sambhaji II was set up at Kolhapur. The Treaty of Warna (1731) recognised two Maratha thrones — Satara and Kolhapur. Shahu Maharaj of Kolhapur (1894–1922) introduced reservations (1902).' }],
  ['Sindhudurg — sea fort',16.042,73.460,'Maharashtra (Sindhudurg, Malvan)','Island fort built by Shivaji (1664–67) — Maratha navy; Shivaji\'s palm & foot prints.','mar',
    { old:'Kurte island; Sindhudurg ("ocean fort"); Malvan',
      more:'Shivaji built this sea fort on Kurte island off Malvan (1664–67), under architect Hiroji Indulkar, to check the Siddis of Janjira and European navies. Lead was used in the foundations. It has a temple with Shivaji\'s image and his palm and foot imprints in lime. The Sindhudurg district is named after it.' }],
  ['Kolaba (Alibag) — Kanhoji Angre',18.634,72.864,'Maharashtra (Raigad)','Sea fort; base of Maratha admiral Kanhoji Angre (early 18th c.) who challenged English, Dutch & Portuguese.','mar',
    { old:'Kolaba / Kulaba fort (Alibag)',
      more:'Sea fort built by Shivaji (1680). Kanhoji Angre, Sarkhel (admiral) of the Maratha navy (c. 1698–1729), made it his base and attacked English, Dutch and Portuguese ships that refused his dastak (pass); the Europeans called him a pirate. His son Tulaji Angre was defeated at Vijaydurg by the British and Peshwa (1756).' }],
  ['Gwalior — Scindia',26.205,78.169,'Madhya Pradesh','Scindia (Ranoji, Mahadji) house; Lashkar camp-city; Mahadji Scindia controlled Mughal Delhi (1771–94).','mar',
    { old:'Gopachala (fort); Lashkar ("camp", Scindia capital, c. 1810)',
      more:'Ranoji Scindia founded the house in Malwa (c. 1731), first based at Ujjain. Mahadji Scindia rebuilt Maratha power after Panipat, restored Shah Alam II in Delhi (1771) and became his regent (vakil-i-mutlaq) until his death (1794). Daulat Rao Scindia moved the capital to the Lashkar camp near Gwalior fort. Rani Lakshmibai died near Gwalior in 1858.' }],
  ['Indore — Holkar (Rajwada)',22.718,75.855,'Madhya Pradesh','Holkar seat founded by Malhar Rao; Ahilyabai Holkar (ruled 1767–95 from Maheshwar) rebuilt Kashi Vishwanath.','mar',
    { old:'Indrapur / Indreshwar (after the Indreshwar temple) → Indore',
      more:'Malhar Rao Holkar, one of Baji Rao I\'s commanders, received Malwa territory (1733). Ahilyabai Holkar ruled 1767–95 from Maheshwar on the Narmada, built ghats and temples across India and rebuilt the Kashi Vishwanath temple (1780). The seven-storey Rajwada palace stands in Indore. Indore was declared India\'s cleanest city several times.' }],
  ['Baroda — Gaekwad',22.294,73.191,'Gujarat (Vadodara)','Gaekwad state (Pilaji Gaekwad, 1721); Sayajirao III; Laxmi Vilas Palace.','mar',
    { old:'Vadapadraka (ancient, "village in banyan grove") → Baroda → Vadodara',
      more:'Pilaji Rao Gaekwad established Maratha power in Gujarat (1721); the Gaekwads became one of the big Maratha houses. Baroda later became a progressive princely state under Sayajirao Gaekwad III (1875–1939), who introduced free compulsory primary education and funded B. R. Ambedkar\'s studies abroad. Laxmi Vilas Palace (1890) is in Indo-Saracenic style.' }],
  ['Nagpur — Bhonsle',21.150,79.081,'Maharashtra','Bhonsles of Nagpur (Raghuji I, 1739) raided Bengal–Orissa (Bargi raids); annexed by Dalhousie 1853.','mar',
    { old:'Nagpur (on the Nag river); earlier Gond kingdom of Deogarh',
      more:'Raghuji I Bhonsle (1739–55) set up a Maratha state at Nagpur after the Gond kings of Deogarh. His Bargi raids devastated Bengal and Orissa; Alivardi Khan ceded Orissa (Cuttack) in 1751. The Bhonsles lost Cuttack and Berar to the British (Treaty of Deogaon, 1803). Nagpur was annexed under the Doctrine of Lapse in 1853–54. Zero Mile marker of British India is here.' }],
  ['Pratapgad (1659)',17.936,73.578,'Maharashtra (Satara)','Shivaji killed Bijapur general Afzal Khan with wagh-nakh (10 Nov 1659).','battle',
    { old:'Pratapgad ("fort of valour"), built 1656 by Moropant Pingle',
      more:'Built for Shivaji by Moropant Pingle (1656) to guard the Par ghat. On 10 November 1659 Shivaji met the Bijapur general Afzal Khan below the fort and killed him with a wagh-nakh (tiger claws) and bichwa; the Maratha army then routed Bijapur\'s forces. Afzal Khan\'s tomb lies below the fort and the Bhavani temple is inside.' }],
  ['Purandar — treaty (1665)',18.283,73.983,'Maharashtra (Pune)','Jai Singh (Mughal) forced Treaty of Purandar — Shivaji ceded 23 of 35 forts; then went to Agra (1666).','battle',
    { old:'Purandar (named after Indra / Purandara)',
      more:'Mughal general Raja Jai Singh of Amber besieged Purandar; on 11 June 1665 Shivaji signed the Treaty of Purandar, giving up 23 of his 35 forts and agreeing to serve the Mughals in the Deccan. He went to Aurangzeb\'s court at Agra (1666), was placed under house arrest and escaped in fruit baskets (trad.). Sambhaji was born at Purandar (1657).' }],
  ['Sinhagad (1670)',18.366,73.755,'Maharashtra (Pune)','Tanaji Malusare recaptured Kondhana from Mughals and died — "Gad ala pan Sinha gela".','battle',
    { old:'Kondhana (earlier name) → Sinhagad ("lion\'s fort")',
      more:'Kondhana had been given to the Mughals at Purandar. On 4 February 1670 Tanaji Malusare scaled its cliff at night (legend says with a monitor lizard, ghorpad) and recaptured it, but died in the fight. Shivaji said "Gad ala, pan sinha gela" (the fort is won, but the lion is gone), and the fort was renamed Sinhagad. Rajaram died here (1700).' }],
  ['Vasai (1739)',19.330,72.815,'Maharashtra (Palghar)','Chimaji Appa captured Bassein from the Portuguese after a long siege.','battle',
    { old:'Baçaim / Bassein (Portuguese) → Vasai',
      more:'Ceded to the Portuguese by Bahadur Shah of Gujarat in the Treaty of Bassein (1534), it was their northern capital with a fort, churches and a Jesuit college. Chimaji Appa, brother of Baji Rao I, captured it after a long siege (February–May 1739). In 1802 Peshwa Baji Rao II signed the Treaty of Bassein accepting British subsidiary alliance here.' }],
  ['Bhopal (1738)',23.259,77.413,'Madhya Pradesh','Baji Rao I defeated Nizam; Convention of Doraha gave Malwa to Marathas.','battle',
    { old:'Bhojpal (trad., after Raja Bhoja\'s dam) → Bhopal',
      more:'In December 1737 Baji Rao I surrounded the Nizam-ul-Mulk\'s army near Bhopal. By the Convention of Doraha (January 1738) the Nizam promised the Marathas the whole of Malwa and territory between the Narmada and Chambal. Bhopal state itself was founded by the Afghan Dost Mohammad Khan (1707) and later ruled by the Begums of Bhopal.' }],
  ['Udgir (1760)',18.396,77.118,'Maharashtra (Latur)','Sadashivrao Bhau defeated Nizam Salabat Jang — peak of Maratha power before Panipat.','battle',
    { old:'Udayagiri (trad.) → Udgir',
      more:'In February 1760 Sadashivrao Bhau defeated Nizam Salabat Jang and his brother Nizam Ali at Udgir. The Treaty of Udgir gave the Marathas large territories, forts like Asirgarh and Daulatabad, and Bijapur region. This was the height of Maratha power, just a year before their defeat at Panipat (1761).' }],
  ['Kharda (1795)',18.646,75.471,'Maharashtra (Ahilyanagar)','Last united Maratha victory (Nana Phadnavis) — Nizam ceded territory & paid tribute.','battle',
    { old:'Kharda / Khardla (Ahmednagar district)',
      more:'In March 1795 a united Maratha army under the Peshwa Sawai Madhavrao, with Nana Phadnavis managing, Daulatrao Scindia, Holkar, Bhonsle and others, defeated Nizam Ali Khan of Hyderabad. The Nizam ceded territory and paid indemnity. It was the last time the Maratha confederacy fought together; the British stayed neutral.' }],
  ['Anandpur Sahib — birth of Khalsa',31.234,76.501,'Punjab (Rupnagar)','Founded by Guru Tegh Bahadur (1665); Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa at Kesgarh, Baisakhi 1699.','sikh',
    { old:'Chakk Nanaki (founded 1665 by Guru Tegh Bahadur) → Anandpur ("city of bliss")',
      more:'Guru Tegh Bahadur bought the land and founded Chakk Nanaki in 1665; he was executed in Delhi in 1675. On Baisakhi 1699 Guru Gobind Singh created the Khalsa at Kesgarh Sahib, initiating the Panj Pyare with amrit and giving the five Ks. Takht Sri Kesgarh Sahib is one of the five Takhts. Hola Mohalla is held here.' }],
  ['Chamkaur (1704)',30.892,76.424,'Punjab (Rupnagar)','Guru Gobind Singh with 40 Sikhs vs Mughals; two elder Sahibzadas killed.','sikh',
    { old:'Chamkaur Sahib (Rupnagar)',
      more:'After leaving Anandpur under a promise of safe passage in December 1704, Guru Gobind Singh was attacked; at Chamkaur he and about 40 Sikhs held a mud fortress (garhi) against a large Mughal army. His two elder sons, Ajit Singh and Jujhar Singh, died fighting. The two younger Sahibzadas were bricked alive at Sirhind. 26 December is observed as Veer Bal Diwas.' }],
  ['Muktsar (Khidrana, 1705)',30.475,74.515,'Punjab','Chali Mukte — 40 liberated ones fell fighting Wazir Khan\'s army; Maghi mela.','sikh',
    { old:'Khidrana (Khidrane di dhab) → Muktsar ("pool of liberation")',
      more:'In December 1705 (some sources say 1705–06) forty Sikhs who had earlier deserted Guru Gobind Singh at Anandpur returned under Mai Bhago and fought Wazir Khan\'s pursuing army at the Khidrana pool. The Guru blessed them as the Chali Mukte ("forty liberated ones") and the place became Muktsar. The Maghi Mela is held here every January.' }],
  ['Talwandi Sabo — Damdama Sahib',29.987,75.079,'Punjab (Bathinda)','Guru Gobind Singh completed the final Adi Granth recension (1705–06); fifth Takht ("Guru ki Kashi").','sikh',
    { old:'Talwandi Sabo; Damdama ("breathing place"); "Guru ki Kashi"',
      more:'Guru Gobind Singh stayed here about nine months (1705–06) after the Battle of Muktsar. With Bhai Mani Singh he prepared the final recension of the Adi Granth, adding Guru Tegh Bahadur\'s hymns (Damdami Bir). It is a centre of Sikh learning and was recognised as the fifth Takht (Takht Sri Damdama Sahib) in 1966.' }],
  ['Chappar Chiri (1710) — Banda Bahadur',30.700,76.667,'Punjab (SAS Nagar)','Banda Singh Bahadur defeated & killed Wazir Khan, then sacked Sirhind; first Sikh state.','sikh',
    { old:'Chappar Chiri (Mohali district)',
      more:'Banda Singh Bahadur, sent to Punjab by Guru Gobind Singh, defeated and killed Wazir Khan, faujdar of Sirhind, at Chappar Chiri on 12 May 1710, avenging the younger Sahibzadas. He then sacked Sirhind. A Fateh Burj, among the tallest victory towers in India, was built here (2011).' }],
  ['Lohgarh — Banda\'s capital',30.429,77.407,'Haryana (Yamunanagar)','Banda Bahadur\'s capital; struck coins in the Gurus\' name; stormed by Bahadur Shah I (Dec 1710).','sikh',
    { old:'Lohgarh ("iron fort") near Mukhlisgarh / Sadhaura',
      more:'Banda Singh Bahadur made the Mughal fort Mukhlisgarh his capital as Lohgarh in the Shivalik foothills. He struck coins in the name of Guru Nanak and Guru Gobind Singh, issued a seal and abolished the zamindari system, giving land to tillers. Bahadur Shah I stormed Lohgarh in December 1710; Banda escaped but was executed in Delhi in 1716.' }],
  ['Amritsar — Harmandir Sahib',31.620,74.877,'Punjab','Guru Ram Das founded Ramdaspur (1577); Arjan Dev built Harmandir, installed Adi Granth (1604); Akal Takht (Hargobind).','sikh',
    { old:'Guru ka Chak / Ramdaspur (Guru Ram Das, 1577) → Amritsar ("pool of nectar")',
      more:'Guru Ram Das founded Ramdaspur (c. 1577) and dug the sacred tank. Guru Arjan Dev built the Harmandir Sahib, had its foundation reportedly laid by the Sufi Mian Mir, and installed the Adi Granth in 1604. Guru Hargobind built the Akal Takht (1606). Ranjit Singh covered the shrine in gold leaf ("Golden Temple"). Operation Blue Star took place here in 1984.' }],
  ['Lahore — Ranjit Singh',31.589,74.311,'Pakistan','Ranjit Singh took Lahore 1799, crowned Maharaja 1801; Treaty of Amritsar 1809 fixed Sutlej boundary; samadhi by fort.','sikh',
    { old:'Lahore (Lohkot, trad.); Sikh capital 1799–1849',
      more:'Ranjit Singh of the Sukerchakia misl captured Lahore in 1799 and was proclaimed Maharaja of Punjab in 1801. By the Treaty of Amritsar (1809) with the British he accepted the Sutlej as his eastern boundary. He built a modern army with European officers (Fauj-i-Khas). His samadhi stands next to the Lahore Fort. The British annexed Punjab in 1849.' }],
  ['Faizabad — early Awadh capital',26.783,82.133,'Uttar Pradesh (Ayodhya)','Awadh Nawabs Safdar Jang & Shuja-ud-Daula ruled from here; Bahu Begum\'s tomb; Saadat Khan founded Awadh 1722.','reg',
    { old:'Bangla (Saadat Khan\'s camp) → Faizabad; renamed Ayodhya district (2018)',
      more:'Saadat Khan Burhan-ul-Mulk, appointed governor of Awadh in 1722, founded the Nawabi dynasty; he built a camp here. Safdar Jang and Shuja-ud-Daula developed Faizabad as capital; Shuja fought at Buxar (1764) and signed the Treaty of Allahabad (1765). The Gulab Bari and Bahu Begum\'s tomb survive. Asaf-ud-Daula moved to Lucknow in 1775.' }],
  ['Lucknow — Awadh Nawabs',26.869,80.913,'Uttar Pradesh','Asaf-ud-Daula shifted capital 1775; Bara Imambara famine-relief work (1784); Rumi Darwaza.','reg',
    { old:'Lakshmanpur / Lakhanpur (traditional) → Lucknow',
      more:'Asaf-ud-Daula moved the Awadh capital from Faizabad to Lucknow in 1775. He built the Bara Imambara (1784) as a famine-relief work, with a vast vaulted hall without beams and the Bhool Bhulaiya maze, and the Rumi Darwaza. Lucknow became a centre of Urdu poetry, Kathak and cuisine. Wajid Ali Shah was deposed in 1856; Begum Hazrat Mahal led the 1857 revolt here.' }],
  ['Hyderabad — Asaf Jahi Nizams',17.358,78.472,'Telangana','Nizam-ul-Mulk Asaf Jah I declared autonomy 1724 (Shakar Kheda); capital moved from Aurangabad 1763.','reg',
    { old:'Bhagnagar (trad., after Bhagmati) → Hyderabad (Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah, 1591); Farkhunda Bunyad',
      more:'Founded in 1591 by Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah near Golconda. Nizam-ul-Mulk Asaf Jah I, a Mughal viceroy, defeated Mubariz Khan at Shakar Kheda (1724) and became virtually independent, ruling from Aurangabad. Nizam Ali Khan moved the capital to Hyderabad in 1763. Hyderabad was the largest princely state; it joined India after Operation Polo (1948).' }],
  ['Murshidabad — Bengal Nawabs',24.187,88.269,'West Bengal','Murshid Quli Khan moved capital from Dhaka (1704/1717); Alivardi, Siraj-ud-Daula; Hazarduari palace (1837).','reg',
    { old:'Makhsusabad / Mukhsudabad → Murshidabad (after Murshid Quli Khan)',
      more:'Murshid Quli Khan, diwan of Bengal, moved his office from Dhaka to Makhsusabad (c. 1704) and renamed it after himself; it became the Nawabi capital (c. 1717). Alivardi Khan and Siraj-ud-Daula ruled from here; the Jagat Seths banked here. After Plassey (1757) Mir Jafar was installed. The Hazarduari palace (1837) and Katra Masjid (Murshid Quli\'s tomb) survive.' }],
  ['Srirangapatna — Hyder & Tipu',12.418,76.695,'Karnataka (Mandya)','Island capital of Hyder Ali & Tipu Sultan on Kaveri; Tipu killed here 1799 (4th Anglo-Mysore War).','reg',
    { old:'Srirangapattana (after the Ranganatha temple); Seringapatam (British)',
      more:'Island town in the Kaveri named after its Ranganathaswamy temple (Adi Ranga). Capital of the Wodeyars from 1610 and of Hyder Ali and Tipu Sultan. Tipu\'s summer palace Dariya Daulat Bagh (1784) and the Gumbaz (tombs) survive. Tipu was killed defending the fort on 4 May 1799 in the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War; the Treaty of Seringapatam (1792) had already cost him half his kingdom.' }],
  ['Arcot — Carnatic Nawabs',12.906,79.319,'Tamil Nadu (Ranipet)','Capital of Nawabs of Carnatic (Saadatullah Khan, Anwaruddin); Clive\'s siege of Arcot (1751).','reg',
    { old:'Arkadu ("six forests", trad.); Arcot',
      more:'Zulfiqar Khan took the region for the Mughals (1690s); Saadatullah Khan made Arcot the capital of the Nawabs of the Carnatic. Anwaruddin died at Ambur (1749). In 1751 Robert Clive seized and held Arcot for 50 days against Chanda Sahib\'s and French forces — a turning point in the Second Carnatic War. The Nawabs later moved to Madras (Chepauk).' }],
  ['Bareilly — Rohilkhand',28.364,79.415,'Uttar Pradesh','Rohilla Afghans (Ali Muhammad Khan, Hafiz Rahmat Khan); Rohilla War 1774; Rampur state founded by Faizullah Khan.','reg',
    { old:'Bans Bareilly (after Bansaldeo and Baraldeo, trad.); Katehr region → Rohilkhand',
      more:'Rohilla Afghans under Ali Muhammad Khan carved out Rohilkhand (1730s–40s) from the Katehr region. Hafiz Rahmat Khan was killed in the Rohilla War (1774) when Warren Hastings lent Company troops to the Nawab of Awadh — later a charge in his impeachment. Faizullah Khan founded the princely state of Rampur. Bareilly was a major centre of the 1857 revolt under Khan Bahadur Khan.' }],
  ['Bharatpur — Lohagarh (Jats)',27.220,77.495,'Rajasthan','Jat ruler Suraj Mal built "iron fort"; resisted Lake\'s siege 1805.','reg',
    { old:'Bharatpur (after Bharata, brother of Rama, trad.); fort called Lohagarh ("iron fort")',
      more:'The Jats rose under Gokula and Rajaram against Aurangzeb; Badan Singh and his son Suraj Mal (d. 1763), the "Plato of the Jats", built a strong Jat kingdom with Bharatpur as capital. Suraj Mal built the mud-walled Lohagarh fort (1733) and briefly took Agra (1761). It withstood Lord Lake\'s four sieges (1805) but fell to Combermere in 1826. Keoladeo National Park is nearby.' }],
  ['Deeg — Jat palaces',27.472,77.324,'Rajasthan (Deeg)','Badan Singh\'s & Suraj Mal\'s summer capital; Jal Mahal palaces with fountains.','reg',
    { old:'Dirgha / Dirghapura (Skanda Purana); Deeg',
      more:'Badan Singh began palaces here and Suraj Mal developed Deeg as a summer capital of the Bharatpur Jats. The Jal Mahal palace complex (Gopal Bhawan, Suraj Bhawan) has gardens, tanks and hundreds of fountains, coloured for the Holi-time festival. Deeg fort has a cannon said to have been taken from Agra. Deeg became a separate district in 2023.' }],
  ['Jaipur — Sawai Jai Singh II',26.925,75.825,'Rajasthan','Planned city founded 1727 by Sawai Jai Singh II (architect Vidyadhar); Jantar Mantar observatory, UNESCO.','reg',
    { old:'Amber / Amer (earlier Kachhwaha capital) → Jaipur (Jainagar, 1727); "Pink City"',
      more:'Sawai Jai Singh II of the Kachhwahas moved his capital from Amber and founded Jaipur in 1727, laid out on a grid by architect Vidyadhar Bhattacharya following Vastu principles. An astronomer, he built five Jantar Mantar observatories (Delhi, Jaipur, Ujjain, Varanasi, Mathura); the Jaipur one is UNESCO (2010), as is the walled city (2019). Painted pink for the Prince of Wales\'s visit (1876).' }],
  ['Udaipur — Mewar capital',24.576,73.683,'Rajasthan','Founded by Udai Singh II (1559) after Chittor; Amar Singh made peace with Jahangir (1615).','reg',
    { old:'Udaipur (founded by Udai Singh II, 1559); "City of Lakes"',
      more:'Rana Udai Singh II founded Udaipur near Lake Pichola in 1559, before Akbar took Chittor (1568). His son Maharana Pratap fought at Haldighati (1576). Amar Singh I made peace with Jahangir through Prince Khurram in 1615 on honourable terms (no personal attendance at court). The City Palace and Lake Palace (Jag Niwas) are famous.' }],
  ['Panna — Chhatrasal Bundela',24.720,80.200,'Madhya Pradesh','Bundela king Chhatrasal\'s capital; Baji Rao I rescued him from Mughals (1729) — Mastani; diamond mines.','reg',
    { old:'Padmavati / Parna (trad.) → Panna',
      more:'Chhatrasal Bundela (1649–1731), inspired by Shivaji and by the Pranami saint Prannath, rebelled against Aurangzeb and founded a Bundela kingdom with Panna as capital. When Muhammad Khan Bangash attacked him (1728–29), Baji Rao I came to his help; Chhatrasal gave him a third of his kingdom and his daughter Mastani. Panna has India\'s only diamond mine (Majhgawan) and a tiger reserve.' }],
  ['Padmanabhapuram — Travancore',8.251,77.327,'Tamil Nadu (Kanyakumari)','Capital of Marthanda Varma, founder of Travancore; Thrippadi Danam (1750) to Padmanabha; capital moved 1795.','reg',
    { old:'Kalkulam (earlier name) → Padmanabhapuram (renamed c. 1744)',
      more:'Capital of Venad/Travancore under Marthanda Varma (1729–58), who crushed feudal lords (Ettuveettil Pillamar) and defeated the Dutch. In 1750 he dedicated the kingdom to Sri Padmanabha (Thrippadi Danam) and ruled as the deity\'s servant (Padmanabha Dasa). The wooden Padmanabhapuram Palace is Kerala-style. The capital moved to Thiruvananthapuram in 1795.' }],
  ['Colachel (1741)',8.179,77.256,'Tamil Nadu (Kanyakumari)','Marthanda Varma defeated the Dutch East India Co.; Dutch captain De Lannoy joined Travancore.','battle',
    { old:'Kolachal / Colachel (Kanyakumari coast)',
      more:'On 10 August 1741 Marthanda Varma of Travancore defeated the Dutch East India Company at Colachel — one of the first defeats of a European power by an Indian state. Captured Dutch officer Eustachius De Lannoy joined Travancore and modernised its army (Nair Brigade) and built Udayagiri fort. The Treaty of Mavelikkara (1753) ended the war.' }],
  ['Rangpur (Sivasagar) — Ahom capital',26.967,94.619,'Assam (Sivasagar)','18th-c. Ahom capital; Rang Ghar amphitheatre (Pramatta Singha, 1746); Ahoms ruled Assam c. 1228–1826.','reg',
    { old:'Rangpur (Ahom capital); Sivasagar ("ocean of Shiva", tank of 1734)',
      more:'The Ahoms, a Tai people led by Sukaphaa, entered Assam in 1228 and ruled for about 600 years. Rudra Singha moved the capital to Rangpur (c. 1707). Pramatta Singha built the Rang Ghar (1746), a two-storey royal pavilion for viewing Bihu games, sometimes called Asia\'s oldest amphitheatre. Talatal Ghar, Kareng Ghar and Sivasagar tank survive. Burmese invasions (1817–26) ended Ahom rule.' }]
 ]
});

